OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026", "title": "A Modulus Proof of Cannon’s Conjecture", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-Modulus-Proof-of-Cannons-Conjecture-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "246", "family_title": "Cannon's conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove that every hyperbolic group whose boundary is homeomorphic to the two-sphere admits a proper cocompact isometric action on hyperbolic three-space with finite kernel. This resolves Cannon's conjecture positively.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1 (Cannon’s conjecture)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a homomorphism \\( \\rho:G\\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Isom}(\\mathbb H^{3}) \\) whose action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) is proper and cocompact.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a hyperbolic group and \\(\\partial G\\) is homeomorphic to \\(S^{2}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An isometric action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) is proper if, for every compact \\(K\\subset\\mathbb H^{3}\\), the set\n  \\[\n  \\{g\\in G:\\rho(g)K\\cap K\\neq\\varnothing\\}\n  \\]\n  is finite.</p><p class=\"li\">The action is cocompact if the translates of one compact set cover \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a homomorphism\n\\[\n\\rho:G\\longrightarrow \\operatorname{Isom}(\\mathbb H^{3})\n\\]\nwhose action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) is proper and cocompact. Its kernel is finite. Orientation-reversing isometries are allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(M(n)\\) were unbounded, the record selection of Proposition 3 would give either exponential records, excluded by Proposition 5, or subexponential growth, excluded by Proposition 7.</p><p>Thus\n\\[\n\\sup_{n}M(n)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nProposition 2 uniformizes the boundary and supplies the proper cocompact isometric action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) with finite kernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Corollary 8.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(G\\) is the fundamental group of a closed hyperbolic three-manifold.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a torsion-free hyperbolic group.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\partial G\\) is homeomorphic to \\(S^{2}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(G\\) is the fundamental group of a closed hyperbolic three-manifold. The manifold may be nonorientable.</p>", "proof": "<p>By Theorem 1, \\(G\\) acts properly and cocompactly on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\). Point stabilizers in a proper action are finite, hence trivial when \\(G\\) is torsion-free.</p><p>Thus the action is free, and\n\\[\n\\mathbb H^{3}/G\n\\]\nis a closed hyperbolic three-manifold with fundamental group \\(G\\). The manifold may be nonorientable.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 8.2 (Virtual structure in the torsion-free case)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Some finite-index subgroup of \\(G\\) is isomorphic to \\( \\pi_{1}(\\Sigma)\\rtimes\\mathbb Z \\) for a closed connected orientable surface \\(\\Sigma\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a torsion-free hyperbolic group.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\partial G\\) is homeomorphic to \\(S^{2}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Some finite-index subgroup of \\(G\\) is isomorphic to\n\\[\n\\pi_{1}(\\Sigma)\\rtimes\\mathbb Z\n\\]\nfor a closed connected orientable surface \\(\\Sigma\\). In particular, \\(G\\) has positive virtual first Betti number. Moreover, \\(G\\) is virtually compact special and residually finite.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(M^{+}\\) be the orientation cover of the closed hyperbolic manifold supplied by Theorem 2, using the manifold itself when it is orientable. Put\n\\[\nG^{+}=\\pi_{1}(M^{+}).\n\\]\nThen \\(G^{+}\\) is normal of index at most two in \\(G\\).</p><p>Agol’s virtual-fibering theorem [1, Theorem 9.2] gives a connected finite cover \\(N\\) of \\(M^{+}\\) fibering over the circle with closed connected orientable fiber \\(\\Sigma\\). The fibration sequence gives\n\\[\n\\pi_{1}(N)\\cong\\pi_{1}(\\Sigma)\\rtimes\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nwith action induced by the monodromy, and its surjection onto \\(\\mathbb Z\\) gives\n\\[\nb_{1}(\\pi_{1}(N))\\ge1.\n\\]</p><p>Bergeron–Wise’s cubulation theorem [5, Theorem 5.3] gives a proper cocompact action of \\(G^{+}\\) on a CAT(0) cube complex, free because \\(G^{+}\\) is torsion-free. Agol’s virtual-special theorem [1, Theorem 1.1] gives a finite-index subgroup of \\(G^{+}\\) with compact special quotient, so \\(G\\) is virtually compact special.</p><p>Agol’s separability corollary [1, Corollary 1.3], applied to the trivial subgroup, makes \\(G^{+}\\) residually finite. The finite quotient \\(G/G^{+}\\), and intersections of the finitely many \\(G\\)-conjugates of finite-index normal subgroups of \\(G^{+}\\), then separate every nontrivial element of \\(G\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The boundary admits the uniform expansion, prefix, packing, and controlled-path estimates established in Lemmas 1.1–1.4.", "statement": "<p>Boundary geometry.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is as in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Gamma\\) is its Cayley graph for a finite symmetric generating set, with unit edge lengths.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(e\\) is the identity vertex and \\(|v|=d_{\\Gamma}(e,v)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The Gromov product is\n  \\[\n  (y|z)_{o}\n  =\\frac12\\bigl(d_{\\Gamma}(o,y)+d_{\\Gamma}(o,z)-d_{\\Gamma}(y,z)\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  Boundary products are defined by limits along rays, up to uniformly bounded additive error.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Z=\\partial G\\), and \\(d\\) is a visual metric satisfying\n  \\[\n  C_{v}^{-1}a^{-(y|z)_{e}}\n  \\le d(y,z)\\le\n  C_{v}a^{-(y|z)_{e}},\n  \\qquad y\\neq z,\n  \\tag{2.1}\n  \\]\n  for some \\(a&gt;1\\). Write \\(r_{k}=a^{-k}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(B(x,r)\\) is open, and\n  \\[\n  \\overline B(x,r)=\\{y:d(x,y)\\le r\\}.\n  \\]\n  A path is a continuous map of a compact interval; a continuum is a nonempty compact connected set.</p><p class=\"li\">For real-valued \\(f\\), write\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{osc}_{A}f=\\sup_{A}f-\\inf_{A}f.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Constants in \\(\\lesssim\\) and \\(\\asymp\\) are independent of the scale indices being varied.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The boundary admits the uniform expansion, prefix, packing, and controlled-path estimates established in Lemmas 1.1–1.4. In particular, \\(Z\\) is doubling. Every fixed \\(g\\in G\\) acts bi-Lipschitzly on \\(Z\\), and the whole boundary action is uniformly quasi-Möbius: there is a single \\(C\\ge1\\) such that\n\\[\nC^{-1}\\frac{d(x,z)d(y,w)}{d(x,w)d(y,z)}\n\\le\n\\frac{d(gx,gz)d(gy,gw)}{d(gx,gw)d(gy,gz)}\n\\le\nC\\frac{d(x,z)d(y,w)}{d(x,w)d(y,z)}\n\\]\nfor every distinct quadruple and every \\(g\\in G\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 1.1–1.4 give the expansion, prefix, packing, and path assertions.</p><p>For a fixed \\(g\\in G\\), changing the basepoint from \\(e\\) to \\(g^{-1}e\\) changes boundary Gromov products by a bounded amount depending on \\(g\\). Thus \\(g\\) acts bi-Lipschitzly.</p><p>For the whole action, the base-change factors cancel in distance cross-ratios. The visual-metric estimates therefore give a single multiplicative cross-ratio constant, independent of \\(g\\), as asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.1 (Expansion formula)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are constants \\(c_{E},C_{E}>0\\), independent of these choices, such that \\( c_{E}\\frac{R\\,d(y,z)}{D_{y}D_{z}} \\le d(hy,hz)\\le C_{E}\\frac{R\\,d(y,z)}{D_{y}D_{z}}. \\) On \\(B(x,CR)\\), \\(h\\) is bi-Lipschitz with scale …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">In the setting of Proposition 1, \\(v\\) is the depth-\\(k\\) vertex on a based ray to \\(x\\in Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  h=v^{-1},\\qquad R=r_{k},\\qquad D_{y}=\\max\\{R,d(x,y)\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are constants \\(c_{E},C_{E}&gt;0\\), independent of these choices, such that\n\\[\nc_{E}\\frac{R\\,d(y,z)}{D_{y}D_{z}}\n\\le d(hy,hz)\\le\nC_{E}\\frac{R\\,d(y,z)}{D_{y}D_{z}}.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nOn \\(B(x,CR)\\), \\(h\\) is bi-Lipschitz with scale factor \\(R^{-1}\\) and distortion depending only on \\(C\\).</p><p>If \\(d(x,z_{0})\\ge\\varepsilon_{*}&gt;0\\), \\(R&lt;\\varepsilon_{*}/2\\), and \\(p=hz_{0}\\), then\n\\[\nd(hy,p)\\lesssim R/D_{y}\\qquad(y\\in Z),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nd(hy,p)\\asymp R/D_{y}\n\\qquad\\bigl(d(x,y)\\le\\varepsilon_{*}/2\\bigr),\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]\nas well as\n\\[\n(v^{-1}|p)_{e}\\ge k-C(\\varepsilon_{*}).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nThe constants in the last estimates may depend on \\(\\varepsilon_{*}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Base change and thin triangles give\n\\[\n(y|z)_{v}\n=(y|z)_{e}+k-(v|y)_{e}-(v|z)_{e}+O(1),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(v|y)_{e}=\\min\\{k,(x|y)_{e}\\}+O(1).\n\\]\nThus \\(a^{-(v|y)_{e}}\\asymp D_{y}\\). Exponentiating the first identity and using\n\\[\n(hy|hz)_{e}=(y|z)_{v}+O(1)\n\\]\nproves (2.2).</p><p>With \\(z=z_{0}\\), the factor \\(D_{z_{0}}\\) is bounded above and below, and \\(d(y,z_{0})\\) is bounded above. When \\(d(x,y)\\le\\varepsilon_{*}/2\\), the latter is also bounded below. This proves (2.3).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n(v^{-1}|v^{-1}z_{0})_{e}\n=k-(v|z_{0})_{e}+O(1),\n\\]\nand \\((v|z_{0})_{e}\\) is bounded in terms of \\(\\varepsilon_{*}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 (Prefixes)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every vertex \\(v\\) lies a uniformly bounded distance from a depth-\\(|v|\\) vertex on a based ray.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The setting is that of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every vertex \\(v\\) lies a uniformly bounded distance from a depth-\\(|v|\\) vertex on a based ray. With that ray’s endpoint as \\(x\\), (2.2) remains valid for\n\\[\nh=v^{-1},\\qquad R=r_{|v|},\n\\]\nwith uniform constants. Moreover:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. If \\(d(\\xi,\\eta)\\le Cr_{j}\\), depth-\\(j\\) vertices on any based rays to \\(\\xi,\\eta\\) are a bounded distance apart, depending only on \\(C\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If \\(|v|=k\\) and \\((v|\\xi)_{e}\\ge k-C\\), every geodesic from \\(e\\) to \\(v\\) stays a bounded synchronous distance from a ray to \\(\\xi\\) through depth \\(k\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Translate a bi-infinite geodesic so that it passes through \\(v\\). The ideal triangle with third vertex \\(e\\) puts \\(v\\) uniformly near one of its based sides; the nearby parameter differs from \\(|v|\\) by a bounded amount.</p><p>A bounded change of basepoint changes products by a bounded amount, preserving (2.2). The two remaining assertions follow from (2.1) and the product estimate\n\\[\n(y|z)_{e}\\ge\\min\\{(y|w)_{e},(w|z)_{e}\\}-O(1).\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.3 (Packing)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(Q\\ge2\\) and \\(C_{P}<\\infty\\) such that an \\(s\\)-separated subset of a ball of radius \\(t\\) has at most \\( C_{P}(t/s)^{Q} \\) points whenever \\( 0<s\\le t\\le\\operatorname{diam}Z. \\) In particular, \\(Z\\) is …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The setting is that of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(Q\\ge2\\) and \\(C_{P}&lt;\\infty\\) such that an \\(s\\)-separated subset of a ball of radius \\(t\\) has at most\n\\[\nC_{P}(t/s)^{Q}\n\\]\npoints whenever\n\\[\n0&lt;s\\le t\\le\\operatorname{diam}Z.\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(Z\\) is doubling.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(k\\) with \\(r_{k}\\asymp t\\). Rays ending in the given ball have their depth-\\(k\\) vertices in a uniformly bounded graph ball, by Lemma 1.2.</p><p>At depth \\(k+j\\) there are at most \\(CA^{j}\\) possible continuations, where \\(A&gt;1\\) depends on the valence. Endpoints whose rays share such a vertex are at visual distance at most \\(C'r_{k+j}\\).</p><p>Choose the least \\(j\\ge0\\) with \\(C'r_{k+j}&lt;s\\). The desired bound follows with\n\\[\nQ\\ge\\max\\{2,\\log_{a}A\\}.\n\\]\nCoarse scales are absorbed by increasing the constant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.4", "orig": "Lemma 2.4 (Controlled paths)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is \\(L\\ge1\\) such that any two points of \\(\\overline B(x,s)\\) join by a path in \\(B(x,Ls)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The setting is that of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is \\(L\\ge1\\) such that any two points of \\(\\overline B(x,s)\\) join by a path in \\(B(x,Ls)\\).</p><p>Any two points of a continuum can also be joined by a path in any prescribed open neighborhood of that continuum.</p>", "proof": "<p>For small \\(s\\), expand at \\(x\\) with \\(R\\asymp s\\) and choose \\(z_{0}\\) a uniformly positive distance from \\(x\\); two fixed distinct points suffice for this choice. By (2.3), the two image points stay a uniform positive distance from \\(p=hz_{0}\\).</p><p>Fix a homeomorphism \\(\\phi:Z\\to S^{2}\\). Uniform continuity of \\(\\phi^{-1}\\) places their spherical images a uniform distance from \\(\\phi(p)\\). Join them outside a smaller round ball about \\(\\phi(p)\\). Uniform continuity of \\(\\phi\\) makes the pullback path stay a uniform \\(d\\)-distance from \\(p\\). The upper bound in (2.3) puts its preimage under \\(h\\) in \\(B(x,Ls)\\).</p><p>Increase \\(L\\) for the remaining scales, using path connectedness of \\(Z\\).</p><p>For a continuum \\(C\\) in an open set \\(V\\), choose \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) with its \\(L\\varepsilon\\)-neighborhood in \\(V\\). Connectedness supplies a finite \\(\\varepsilon\\)-chain in \\(C\\) between any two specified points: the set reachable by such chains is relatively clopen. Join consecutive points by the first assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If \\( \\sup_{n}M(n)<\\infty, \\) then \\((Z,d)\\) is quasi-Möbius homeomorphic to the round sphere, and \\(G\\) admits a proper cocompact isometric action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) with finite kernel.", "statement": "<p>The classical modulus reduction and realization in hyperbolic space.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G,Z,d,a,r_{n}\\) are as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">For each \\(n\\ge0\\), choose a maximal \\(r_{n}\\)-separated set \\(T_{n}\\subset Z\\), with cells\n  \\[\n  B(q,2r_{n}),\\qquad q\\in T_{n}.\n  \\]\n  These cover \\(Z\\); fixed enlargements have bounded overlap.</p><p class=\"li\">For a path family \\(\\mathcal F\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Mod}_{2}(\\mathcal F,T_{n})\n  =\n  \\min_{\\omega\\ge0}\n  \\left\\{\n  \\sum_{q\\in T_{n}}\\omega(q)^{2}:\n  \\sum_{B(q,2r_{n})\\cap\\pi\\neq\\varnothing}\\omega(q)\\ge1\n  \\text{ for every }\\pi\\in\\mathcal F\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]\n  Only finitely many incidence vectors occur, so the minimum exists.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix sufficiently small \\(d_{0}&gt;0\\), with \\(d_{0}&lt;\\operatorname{diam}Z/10\\), and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal F_{0}=\\{\\pi:\\operatorname{diam}\\pi\\ge d_{0}\\},\n  \\qquad\n  M(n)=\\operatorname{Mod}_{2}(\\mathcal F_{0},T_{n}).\n  \\tag{2.5}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(d_{0}\\) so that every path of diameter at least \\(R/2\\) in \\(\\overline B(x,3R)\\) has image diameter at least \\(d_{0}\\) under a scale-\\(R\\) expansion. Also impose the smallness requirement of Lemma 2.1 below.</p><p class=\"li\">The family \\(\\mathcal F_{0}\\) is nonempty, and\n  \\[\n  0&lt;M(n)&lt;\\infty.\n  \\]\n  All-one weights are admissible, while any one path gives the lower bound \\(1/\\#T_{n}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\n\\sup_{n}M(n)&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nthen \\((Z,d)\\) is quasi-Möbius homeomorphic to the round sphere, and \\(G\\) admits a proper cocompact isometric action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) with finite kernel.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 gives the quasi-Möbius parametrization under the uniform modulus bound. Lemma 2.2 then supplies the proper cocompact isometric action with finite kernel.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Theorem 2.5 (Bonk–Kleiner; Bourdon–Kleiner)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\( \\sup_{n}M(n)<\\infty, \\) then \\((Z,d)\\) is quasi-Möbius homeomorphic to the round sphere.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The boundary, approximating covers, sufficiently small \\(d_{0}\\), and moduli \\(M(n)\\) are those of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\n\\sup_{n}M(n)&lt;\\infty,\n\\]\nthen \\((Z,d)\\) is quasi-Möbius homeomorphic to the round sphere.</p>", "proof": "<p>Corollary 3.5 of Bourdon–Kleiner [8], derived from Bonk–Kleiner [6, Theorem 10.4], applies to an arcwise connected, approximately self-similar metric sphere.</p><p>Approximate self-similarity means that each open ball, with distances divided by its radius, is uniformly bi-Lipschitz to an open subset of the space. Proposition 1 supplies this property through the expansion formula; intermediate and large radii change only the constant.</p><p>The criterion assumes bounded \\(2\\)-moduli of paths of diameter at least a sufficiently small \\(d_{0}\\) in successive graph approximations. For a dyadic scale \\(2^{-m}\\), choose \\(n\\) with\n\\[\n2^{-m}/a&lt;r_{n}\\le2^{-m}.\n\\]\nThe cells form a fixed \\(\\kappa\\)-approximation for any \\(\\kappa&gt;2a\\): the inner balls of radius \\(2^{-m}/\\kappa\\) are disjoint, each is contained in its cell, and each cell is contained in the radius-\\(\\kappa2^{-m}\\) ball about its center.</p><p>The modulus is exactly \\(M(n)\\). Finitely many coarse scales have finite modulus. No Coxeter-group assumption occurs in this criterion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Proposition 2.6 (From the boundary to hyperbolic space)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(G\\) admits a proper cocompact isometric action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) with finite kernel.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G\\) is a hyperbolic group with sphere boundary \\(Z\\), equipped with a visual metric \\(d\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\((Z,d)\\) is quasi-Möbius homeomorphic to the round sphere.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(G\\) admits a proper cocompact isometric action on \\(\\mathbb H^{3}\\) with finite kernel.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\n\\psi:Z\\longrightarrow S^{2}\n\\]\nbe the uniformizing homeomorphism and write\n\\[\n\\mathcal G=\\{\\psi g\\psi^{-1}:g\\in G\\}.\n\\]\nThe boundary cross-ratio estimate and the two fixed maps \\(\\psi,\\psi^{-1}\\) give one quasi-Möbius distortion function for every element of \\(\\mathcal G\\).</p><p>Fix \\(f\\in\\mathcal G\\) and \\(x\\in S^{2}\\), keep a fourth point away from \\(x\\), and let two points at equal spherical distance from \\(x\\) approach \\(x\\). In the cross-ratio inequality the factors involving the fourth point tend to one, both before and after applying \\(f\\). Thus the infinitesimal linear distortion has a bound independent of \\(f\\) and \\(x\\); the radius at which the estimate is accurate need not be uniform in \\(f\\).</p><p>The metric characterization of planar quasiconformality, applied in conformal charts, makes each \\(f\\) quasiconformal. At its almost-everywhere nonsingular differentiability points, the same infinitesimal bound controls the ratio of singular values of \\(Df\\), and hence its analytic dilatation. This gives a common quasiconformal bound. For a reversing map, apply the characterization after a conformal reflection; see [4, Theorems 2.5.3–2.5.4] and [17, Theorem 1.4 and Remark 1.8(b)].</p><p>Use the invariant-conformal-structure method of Sullivan, with Tukia’s circumcenter construction [24, pp. 73–76]. Analytic quasiconformal maps have almost-everywhere defined nonsingular weak differentials, and they and their inverses preserve null sets [4, Theorem 3.1.2]. Their differentials satisfy the almost-everywhere composition rule [2, Lemma 10 and Theorem 5].</p><p>Since \\(\\mathcal G\\) is countable, remove the \\(\\mathcal G\\)-orbit of the union of all exceptional sets for these assertions and for all pairs of maps. Its complement \\(X\\) is invariant and conull.</p><p>At \\(x\\in X\\), let \\(\\mathcal C_{x}\\) be the space of positive definite quadratic forms on \\(T_{x}S^{2}\\), modulo positive scalars. This is a hyperbolic plane, and pullback by any invertible linear map, of either orientation, is an isometry between the corresponding fibers [4, Section 2.3].</p><p>The orbit\n\\[\n\\{f^{*}q_{\\mathrm{round}}(x):f\\in\\mathcal G\\}\\subset\\mathcal C_{x}\n\\]\nis uniformly bounded by the common quasiconformal bound and contains the round form. Its unique hyperbolic circumcenter defines a uniformly bounded measurable conformal structure \\(q(x)\\).</p><p>Indeed, circumcenters of the increasing finite subsets from an enumeration are measurable and converge to that circumcenter: their radii increase, their centers stay in a fixed compact ball, and every subsequential limit is the unique center of the whole orbit. The composition rule permutes this orbit under pullback, so\n\\[\nf^{*}q=q\n\\]\non \\(X\\) for every \\(f\\in\\mathcal G\\). Extend \\(q\\) arbitrarily across the null complement of \\(X\\).</p><p>The measurable Riemann mapping theorem [2, Theorem 6] gives a quasiconformal homeomorphism\n\\[\nF:S^{2}\\longrightarrow S^{2},\n\\qquad\nF^{*}q_{\\mathrm{round}}=q\n\\]\nalmost everywhere. Invariance and the analytic composition rule show that every \\(FfF^{-1}\\) is conformal or anticonformal, according to the orientation of \\(f\\). Such sphere homeomorphisms are Möbius or anti-Möbius. Their unique extensions to hyperbolic isometries define\n\\[\n\\rho:G\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Isom}(\\mathbb H^{3}).\n\\]</p><p>Properness and cocompactness follow directly from the action on ordered distinct triples \\(T(Z)\\). On a compact set of triples all pairwise distances are bounded below. For \\(|v|\\to\\infty\\), at least two points of such a triple stay away from the expansion center for \\(v^{-1}\\); their image distance is \\(O(r_{|v|})\\). Thus\n\\[\nv^{-1}K\\cap K=\\varnothing\n\\]\neventually for every compact \\(K\\subset T(Z)\\).</p><p>Conversely, expand at one point of a closest pair with scale comparable to their distance. Formula (2.2) makes all three image distances uniformly positive: if the third point is nearby, all denominator factors have that scale; otherwise its distances to the pair cancel its denominator factor. Triples already uniformly separated need no expansion. Hence the action on triples is proper and cocompact.</p><p>These properties survive boundary conjugacy. Let\n\\[\nI=\\operatorname{Isom}(\\mathbb H^{3}).\n\\]\nThe stabilizer \\(H\\) of a standard ordered boundary triple has two elements: the identity and reflection in its hyperbolic plane. Choose \\(o\\in\\mathbb H^{3}\\) fixed by \\(H\\), and let \\(K_{o}\\) be its compact stabilizer in \\(I\\). The equivariant map\n\\[\nT(S^{2})\\cong I/H\\longrightarrow I/K_{o}\\cong\\mathbb H^{3}\n\\]\nis continuous, onto, and proper: isometries sending \\(o\\) into a compact set form a compact set. Properness and cocompactness therefore descend. The kernel fixes \\(o\\), so is finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 3.7 (Scalar limit), with the preceding scalar construction", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Along a subsequence of the record indices, \\( H_{N}\\longrightarrow u \\) locally uniformly on \\(U\\), and \\( \\mu_{N}\\rightharpoonup\\mu, \\) where \\(u:U\\to[0,1]\\) is continuous and nonconstant and \\(\\mu\\) is finite.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the boundary, covers, and moduli of Propositions 1 and 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(M(n)\\) is unbounded.</p><p class=\"li\">If\n  \\[\n  \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}M(n)^{1/n}&gt;1,\n  \\]\n  choose\n  \\[\n  1&lt;\\lambda&lt;\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}M(n)^{1/n};\n  \\]\n  otherwise put \\(\\lambda=1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In both cases \\(M(n)/\\lambda^{n}\\) is unbounded. Retain increasing record indices \\(N\\) whose record values tend to infinity. Then\n  \\[\n  M(N)\\to\\infty,\n  \\qquad\n  M(N-l)\\le\\lambda^{-l}M(N)\n  \\quad(0\\le l\\le N).\n  \\tag{3.1}\n  \\]\n  This inequality survives further subsequences.</p><p class=\"li\">The coordinate rectangles\n  \\[\n  Q=[0,1]^{2}\\subset\\widehat Q=[0,1]\\times[-1,2]\\subset Z\n  \\]\n  are supplied by Lemma 3.1. Put\n  \\[\n  U=\\operatorname{int}_{Z}\\widehat Q.\n  \\]\n  The edge \\(y=0\\) is the top of \\(Q\\). The rectangles\n  \\[\n  [0,1]\\times[-1,0],\n  \\qquad\n  [0,1]\\times[1,2]\n  \\]\n  are the top and bottom bands.</p><p class=\"li\">For the dual weights \\(\\rho_{q}^{(N)}\\) and continuous regularizations \\(H_{N}\\) constructed in Lemmas 3.3 and 3.4, put\n  \\[\n  \\mu_{N}\n  =\n  M(N)\\sum_{q\\in T_{N}}(\\rho_{q}^{(N)})^{2}\\delta_{q}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For continuous \\(f\\) on an open set containing compact \\(K\\), and \\(J\\ge2\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E_{n}^{(J)}(f;K)\n  =\n  \\sum_{\\substack{q\\in T_{n}\\\\B(q,2r_{n})\\cap K\\neq\\varnothing}}\n  \\bigl(\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,Jr_{n})}f\\bigr)^{2}.\n  \\]\n  The closed balls lie in the domain for sufficiently large \\(n\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Along a subsequence of the record indices,\n\\[\nH_{N}\\longrightarrow u\n\\]\nlocally uniformly on \\(U\\), and\n\\[\n\\mu_{N}\\rightharpoonup\\mu,\n\\]\nwhere \\(u:U\\to[0,1]\\) is continuous and nonconstant and \\(\\mu\\) is finite.</p><p>The function \\(u\\) is zero and one on the interiors of the respective bands. If \\(\\lambda&gt;1\\), it is locally Hölder continuous.</p><p>For every compact \\(K\\subset U\\), and sufficiently small \\(s&gt;0\\), uniformly for \\(x\\in K\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(x,s)}u\\bigr)^{2}\n\\le\nC\\mu\\bigl(\\overline B(x,C_{\\mu}s)\\bigr).\n\\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\), there is a continuum \\(C_{t}\\subset Q\\) of diameter at least a fixed \\(d_{1}&gt;0\\), with \\(u=t\\) on \\(C_{t}\\cap U\\), meeting every compact path in \\(U\\) whose endpoint values strictly straddle \\(t\\).</p><p>For every fixed \\(g\\in G\\), compact \\(K'\\subset gU\\), and fixed \\(J\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{N}^{(J)}(H_{N}\\circ g^{-1};K')\n\\le\n\\frac{C(g,K',J)}{M(N)}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix compact \\(K\\subset U\\), and let \\(x,y\\in K\\) with \\(d(x,y)\\le r_{k}\\). By Proposition 1, they join inside \\(B(x,Lr_{k})\\).</p><p>Choose \\(b\\) with \\(a^{b}&gt;2L\\), and consider indices \\(l\\le k-b\\) above a fixed interior threshold, in an arithmetic progression of step \\(b_{1}\\) with \\(a^{b_{1}}&gt;16\\). The annuli\n\\[\n\\{r_{l}/4\\le d(x,\\cdot)\\le4r_{l}\\}\n\\]\nare disjoint.</p><p>For \\(N\\) sufficiently large relative to \\(k\\), Lemma 3.6 and (3.1) give\n\\[\n|H_{N}(x)-H_{N}(y)|^{2}\n\\le\nC\\lambda^{-l}\n\\mu_{N}\\{r_{l}/4\\le d(x,\\cdot)\\le4r_{l}\\}.\n\\]\nThe total masses of \\(\\mu_{N}\\) are uniformly bounded by Lemma 3.3. Summing over at least \\(ck\\) indices yields\n\\[\n|H_{N}(x)-H_{N}(y)|\\le C_{K}k^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\lambda&gt;1\\), the single choice \\(l=k-b\\) gives \\(C_{K}\\lambda^{-k/2}\\) instead.</p><p>For each tolerance this controls a tail uniformly; the finitely many omitted functions are individually uniformly continuous. Thus the sequence is equicontinuous on \\(K\\).</p><p>Arzelà–Ascoli on a compact exhaustion of \\(U\\), followed by weak compactness of the measures on \\(Z\\), gives a subsequence with\n\\[\nH_{N}\\to u\n\\]\nlocally uniformly and \\(\\mu_{N}\\rightharpoonup\\mu\\). The two constant band values prove nonconstancy. The stronger estimate gives local Hölder exponent\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log\\lambda}{2\\log a}.\n\\]</p><p>For (3.6), fix \\(A&gt;2L\\) and choose\n\\[\nAs\\le R=r_{l}&lt;aAs.\n\\]\nAny two points of \\(\\overline B(x,s)\\) join in\n\\[\nB(x,Ls)\\subset B(x,R/2).\n\\]\nThe annular and record estimates give\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(x,s)}H_{N}\\bigr)^{2}\n\\le\nC\\mu_{N}\\bigl(\\overline B(x,4aAs)\\bigr)\n\\]\nfor large \\(N\\).</p><p>Local uniform convergence passes the oscillation to the limit; the closed-set inequality for weak convergence bounds the right-hand limsup by\n\\[\nC\\mu\\bigl(\\overline B(x,4aAs)\\bigr).\n\\]\nTake \\(C_{\\mu}=4aA\\).</p><p>For each fixed \\(t\\), take a Hausdorff limit \\(C_{t}\\) of \\(C_{N,t}\\) in \\(Q\\). It is connected and has diameter at least \\(d_{1}\\). Local uniform convergence gives \\(u=t\\) on \\(C_{t}\\cap U\\). A fixed compact straddling path also straddles \\(t\\) for every sufficiently large \\(H_{N}\\), hence meets those \\(C_{N,t}\\) and their limit. The subsequence used here may depend on \\(t\\); no simultaneous selection is needed.</p><p>The fixed-transform energy assertion is Lemma 3.5.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.1 (Localization to a rectangle)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are coordinate rectangles \\( Q=[0,1]^{2}\\subset\\widehat Q=[0,1]\\times[-1,2]\\subset Z \\) and a subsequence of the record indices such that the left-to-right crossing modulus of \\(Q\\) is at least \\( cM(N), \\) where …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the unbounded-modulus and record-index setting of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are coordinate rectangles\n\\[\nQ=[0,1]^{2}\\subset\\widehat Q=[0,1]\\times[-1,2]\\subset Z\n\\]\nand a subsequence of the record indices such that the left-to-right crossing modulus of \\(Q\\) is at least\n\\[\ncM(N),\n\\]\nwhere \\(c&gt;0\\) is independent of \\(N\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Cover \\(Z\\) by finitely many coordinate boxes \\([-1,1]^{2}\\), each lying in a chart containing \\([-8,8]^{2}\\) of diameter less than \\(d_{0}\\).</p><p>A macroscopic path meeting an inner box must leave its larger box. Follow it to its first meeting with \\(\\partial[-2,2]^{2}\\). If it exits through \\(x=2\\), its segment after the last preceding meeting with \\(x=1\\) crosses\n\\[\n[1,2]\\times[-2,2]\n\\]\nfrom left to right. The other three exit sides give analogous strips. Each strip extends by its height in both transverse directions inside \\([-8,8]^{2}\\).</p><p>Consequently there are finitely many crossing families\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{1},\\ldots,\\mathcal F_{J}\n\\]\nsuch that every macroscopic path contains a member of one of them. Summing admissible weights gives\n\\[\nM(N)\n\\le\nJ\\sum_{j=1}^{J}\\operatorname{Mod}_{2}(\\mathcal F_{j},T_{N})\n\\le\nJ^{2}\\max_{j}\\operatorname{Mod}_{2}(\\mathcal F_{j},T_{N}).\n\\]\nOne strip realizes this bound along a subsequence. Affine coordinates give the asserted rectangles.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 (Crossings and blockers)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every continuum meeting both lateral edges meets every top-to-bottom path.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in a closed coordinate rectangle.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every continuum meeting both lateral edges meets every top-to-bottom path.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Every compact set meeting all top-to-bottom paths contains a continuum meeting both lateral edges.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the first assertion, suppose a lateral continuum and a top-to-bottom path are disjoint. Enlarge the rectangle, extending the continuum horizontally and the path vertically to the corresponding new edges. The extensions remain disjoint: a new horizontal and vertical segment can meet only at an original corner, already a common point of the original sets.</p><p>Approximate the extended path, within its positive clearance from the continuum, by a polygonal path with interior in the enlarged rectangle. After subdivision at intersections it contains a simple polygonal arc between the top and bottom edges. The Jordan curve theorem, applied after adjoining a boundary route, shows that this arc separates the lateral edges. The connected continuum cannot avoid it.</p><p>For the second assertion, subdivide into finer square grids and mark the closed squares meeting the blocker \\(K\\). No edge-adjacent chain of unmarked squares joins top to bottom.</p><p>Label \\(A\\) the unmarked squares reachable from the top through unmarked edge-adjacent squares, and append a top row of \\(A\\) squares. Label all remaining squares and an appended bottom row \\(B\\). Draw the interfaces between unlike labels. Interior vertices have even degree; each lateral boundary has an odd number of ends, since its labels begin with \\(A\\) and end with \\(B\\). There are no top or bottom ends.</p><p>Some interface component therefore joins the lateral boundaries: otherwise every component contributes an even number of ends to the only lateral boundary it meets.</p><p>Along a path in this component, the adjacent \\(B\\) squares are original marked squares. An adjacent unmarked square would be reachable, while an interface with the appended bottom row would give an unmarked top-to-bottom chain. Successive \\(B\\) squares meet at least at a corner. Their union is a connected compact lateral crossing.</p><p>A Hausdorff limit as the mesh tends to zero is connected, meets both lateral edges, and lies in \\(K\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Crossing dual weights)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For each retained \\(N\\), there are nonnegative weights \\( \\rho_{q}=\\rho_{q}^{(N)},\\qquad q\\in T_{N}, \\) admissible for top-to-bottom crossings of \\(Q\\), such that \\( \\sum_{q\\in T_{N}}\\rho_{q}^{2} \\le \\frac{C}{M(N)}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the rectangle and retained record indices from Lemma 3.1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For each retained \\(N\\), there are nonnegative weights\n\\[\n\\rho_{q}=\\rho_{q}^{(N)},\\qquad q\\in T_{N},\n\\]\nadmissible for top-to-bottom crossings of \\(Q\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{q\\in T_{N}}\\rho_{q}^{2}\n\\le\n\\frac{C}{M(N)}.\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(P\\) minimize Euclidean norm in the convex hull of the finitely many cell-incidence vectors \\(v\\) of left-to-right crossings. Its coordinates are nonnegative and sum to at least one, so \\(P\\neq0\\).</p><p>Minimality gives\n\\[\n\\langle P,v\\rangle\\ge\\|P\\|^{2},\n\\]\nmaking \\(P/\\|P\\|^{2}\\) admissible. Conversely, any admissible \\(\\omega\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\langle\\omega,P\\rangle\\ge1.\n\\]\nThus the left-to-right modulus is \\(\\|P\\|^{-2}\\).</p><p>By Lemma 3.2, each top-to-bottom incidence vector has inner product at least one with every \\(v\\), hence with \\(P\\). Set \\(\\rho=P\\) and apply Lemma 3.1.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.4 (Regularization and level continua)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The functions \\(F_{N},H_{N}\\) are continuous, with the prescribed band values, and for every path \\(K\\subset\\widehat Q\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use \\(Q,\\widehat Q\\) and the dual weights \\(\\rho_{q}\\) from Lemma 3.3.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\pi\\subset\\widehat Q\\), set\n  \\[\n  \\ell_{\\rho}(\\pi)\n  =\n  \\sum_{q:B(q,2r_{N})\\cap\\pi\\neq\\varnothing}\\rho_{q}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x\\in Q\\), let \\(b_{N}(x)\\) be the infimum of this cost over paths from the top edge to \\(x\\) in \\(Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define the raw function \\(\\min\\{1,b_{N}\\}\\), assigning exactly zero on the top edge, and extend it by zero and one to the top and bottom bands. Admissibility gives value one on the bottom edge.</p><p class=\"li\">Triangulate \\(\\widehat Q\\) by a finite coordinate grid containing the band boundaries, with every triangle of \\(d\\)-diameter less than \\(r_{N}\\). Affinely interpolate the raw vertex values to obtain\n  \\[\n  U_{N}^{0}:\\widehat Q\\to[0,1].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define the path envelopes\n  \\[\n  F_{N}(x)\n  =\n  \\inf_{\\pi:x\\to\\mathrm{top}}\\max_{\\pi}U_{N}^{0},\n  \\qquad\n  H_{N}(x)\n  =\n  \\sup_{\\pi:x\\to\\mathrm{bottom}}\\inf_{\\pi}F_{N},\n  \\]\n  where paths remain in \\(\\widehat Q\\) and end in the indicated band.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The functions \\(F_{N},H_{N}\\) are continuous, with the prescribed band values, and for every path \\(K\\subset\\widehat Q\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{K}H_{N}\n\\le\n\\operatorname{osc}_{K}F_{N}\n\\le\n\\operatorname{osc}_{K}U_{N}^{0}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(0&lt;t&lt;1\\), the set\n\\[\n\\{H_{N}=t\\}\\cap Q\n\\]\ncontains a lateral crossing continuum \\(C_{N,t}\\) of diameter at least a fixed \\(d_{1}&gt;0\\). Every path in \\(\\widehat Q\\) whose endpoint values strictly straddle \\(t\\) meets \\(C_{N,t}\\).</p><p>The raw function also has the endpoint-cost property: along every path in \\(\\widehat Q\\), the difference of its raw endpoint values is at most the path’s \\(\\ell_{\\rho}\\)-cost.</p>", "proof": "<p>First verify the raw endpoint-cost property. Inside \\(Q\\), concatenate with nearly minimizing paths and clip at one; a path beginning on the top edge is itself a competitor.</p><p>A path visiting both bands contains a crossing of cost at least one. If it visits only the top band, each endpoint value is bounded by the cost of its segment to that band. If it visits only the bottom band, each endpoint value is at least one minus that segment’s cost, by concatenation with a top-to-endpoint competitor. In either case the endpoint difference is bounded by the cost of the entire path.</p><p>The interpolant \\(U_{N}^{0}\\) is continuous and constant on the bands. For the envelopes,\n\\[\nF_{N}\\ge U_{N}^{0},\n\\qquad\nH_{N}\\le F_{N};\n\\]\nconstant paths give the band values.</p><p>Concatenation with nearly optimizing paths gives, for \\(x,y\\in K\\),\n\\[\nF_{N}(x)\n\\le\n\\max\\{\\max_{K}U_{N}^{0},F_{N}(y)\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nH_{N}(y)\n\\ge\n\\min\\{\\inf_{K}F_{N},H_{N}(x)\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(F_{N}(x)&gt;F_{N}(y)\\), the first inequality places their values between \\(\\min_{K}U_{N}^{0}\\) and \\(\\max_{K}U_{N}^{0}\\). If \\(H_{N}(x)&gt;H_{N}(y)\\), the second places them between \\(\\inf_{K}F_{N}\\) and \\(\\sup_{K}F_{N}\\). This proves (3.3).</p><p>Short coordinate segments and continuity of \\(U_{N}^{0}\\) then prove continuity of both functions, including at the boundary.</p><p>Every point of \\(\\{H_{N}&gt;t\\}\\) joins the bottom band within that set: choose a path with \\(\\inf_{\\pi}F_{N}&gt;t\\) and use its tails. Likewise every point of \\(\\{F_{N}&lt;t\\}\\) joins the top within \\(\\{F_{N}&lt;t\\}\\).</p><p>To connect a point \\(x\\) with\n\\[\nH_{N}(x)&lt;t\\le F_{N}(x)\n\\]\nto the top, choose\n\\[\nH_{N}(x)&lt;s&lt;t\n\\]\nand follow any top-going path until its first point with \\(F_{N}=s\\). On this prefix \\(F_{N}\\ge s\\), so concatenation with nearly maximizing paths from any prefix point \\(z\\) gives\n\\[\nH_{N}(x)\\ge\\min\\{s,H_{N}(z)\\}.\n\\]\nHence \\(H_{N}(z)\\le H_{N}(x)&lt;t\\) throughout the prefix. Its endpoint joins the top through \\(F_{N}&lt;t\\), hence through \\(H_{N}&lt;t\\).</p><p>Thus both strict sides of the level join their respective bands, and then the outer top and bottom edges, without meeting the level.</p><p>The compact set \\(\\{H_{N}=t\\}\\) blocks all top-to-bottom paths by continuity and lies in \\(Q\\). By Lemma 3.2 it contains a lateral continuum \\(C_{N,t}\\). Take \\(d_{1}\\) to be the distance between the lateral edges of \\(Q\\).</p><p>Complete any straddling path to a top-to-bottom path using the strict-side connections. The completed path meets \\(C_{N,t}\\) by Lemma 3.2, and the added pieces avoid the level. Therefore the original straddling path meets \\(C_{N,t}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "Lemma 3.5 (Local oscillation)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Uniformly for \\(x\\in K\\) and sufficiently large \\(N\\), \\( \\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(x,Jr_{N})}H_{N} \\le \\sum_{\\substack{q\\in T_{N}\\\\d(q,x)\\le C(J)r_{N}}}\\rho_{q}. \\) Here \\(C(J)\\) depends only on \\(J\\) and the …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the scalar construction of Lemmas 3.3 and 3.4.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(U=\\operatorname{int}_{Z}\\widehat Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\subset U\\) is compact and \\(J&gt;0\\) is fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Uniformly for \\(x\\in K\\) and sufficiently large \\(N\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(x,Jr_{N})}H_{N}\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\substack{q\\in T_{N}\\\\d(q,x)\\le C(J)r_{N}}}\\rho_{q}.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(C(J)\\) depends only on \\(J\\) and the ball path constant.</p><p>Consequently, for every fixed \\(g\\in G\\), compact \\(K'\\subset gU\\), and fixed \\(J\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{N}^{(J)}(H_{N}\\circ g^{-1};K')\n\\le\n\\frac{C(g,K',J)}{M(N)}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Join two points of \\(\\overline B(x,Jr_{N})\\) inside \\(B(x,LJr_{N})\\), using Proposition 1.</p><p>Vertices of triangles meeting this path lie in\n\\[\nB(x,(LJ+1)r_{N}).\n\\]\nAny two such vertices join inside\n\\[\nB(x,L(LJ+1)r_{N})\\subset U\n\\]\nfor large \\(N\\).</p><p>The raw endpoint inequality bounds every difference of their values by the right side of (3.4), with\n\\[\nC(J)=L(LJ+1)+3.\n\\]\nIt therefore bounds the interpolant’s oscillation along the original path. Apply (3.3).</p><p>Squaring and summing costs a bounded factor by the packing estimate: both the number of nearby weights and the number of centers charging each weight are bounded. The same holds for \\(br_{N}\\)-separated centers, with constants depending on \\(b&gt;0\\).</p><p>A fixed \\(g\\) is bi-Lipschitz, so inverse images of target balls lie in source balls of radius \\(C_{g}Jr_{N}\\), their centers are \\(b_{g}r_{N}\\)-separated, and all these source balls lie in a fixed compact subset of \\(U\\). The summation bound and (3.2) finish the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.6", "orig": "Lemma 3.6 (Annular estimate)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are \\(R_{*}>0\\) and an integer \\(m_{0}\\) such that, if \\( x\\in K,\\qquad R=r_{l}\\le R_{*},\\qquad N-l\\ge m_{0}, \\) then any two points joined by a path in \\(B(x,R/2)\\) have \\(H_{N}\\)-value difference \\(\\Delta\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the scalar construction and record indices of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(K\\subset U\\) is compact.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(R_{*}&gt;0\\) and an integer \\(m_{0}\\) such that, if\n\\[\nx\\in K,\\qquad R=r_{l}\\le R_{*},\\qquad N-l\\ge m_{0},\n\\]\nthen any two points joined by a path in \\(B(x,R/2)\\) have \\(H_{N}\\)-value difference \\(\\Delta\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\Delta^{2}\n\\le\nCM(N-l)\n\\sum_{\\substack{q\\in T_{N}\\\\R/4\\le d(q,x)\\le4R}}\\rho_{q}^{2}.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThe constant is uniform; \\(N\\) is sufficiently large for the fixed interior region.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(R_{*}\\) with\n\\[\n4R_{*}&lt;d_{1}\n\\]\nand all closed \\(5R_{*}\\)-balls about \\(K\\) contained in \\(U\\). All following restrictions on \\(m_{0}\\) concern only\n\\[\nr_{N}/R=a^{-(N-l)}.\n\\]</p><p>Expand at \\(x,l\\) by \\(h\\), and choose macroscopic admissible weights \\(\\omega\\) at scale \\(N-l\\) with squared mass \\(M(N-l)\\). Put\n\\[\n\\mathcal A\n=\n\\{q\\in T_{N}:R/2\\le d(q,x)\\le3R\\},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sigma_{q}\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\xi\\in T_{N-l}\\\\d(\\xi,hq)\\le C_{0}r_{N-l}}}\\omega_{\\xi},\n\\qquad q\\in\\mathcal A,\n\\]\nwhere \\(C_{0}&gt;C_{E}+2\\).</p><p>Expansion on \\(B(x,3R)\\) separates the \\(hq\\) by a fixed multiple of \\(r_{N-l}\\). Packing bounds both overlap multiplicities, giving\n\\[\n\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal A}\\sigma_{q}^{2}\n\\le CM(N-l).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(I\\) be the open interval between the endpoint values. For \\(t\\in I\\), the continuum \\(C_{N,t}\\) meets the given path and exits \\(B(x,2R)\\), since its diameter exceeds \\(4R\\).</p><p>Join these two continuum points in its \\(r_{N}/4\\)-neighborhood, using Proposition 1, and retain a radial subpath \\(\\eta_{t}\\) between radii \\(R\\) and \\(2R\\), contained in that closed annulus. Its diameter is at least \\(R\\), so \\(h\\eta_{t}\\) is macroscopic by the choice of \\(d_{0}\\).</p><p>If a target cell \\(B(\\xi,2r_{N-l})\\) meets \\(h\\eta_{t}\\) at \\(hy\\), choose \\(q\\in T_{N}\\) with \\(d(q,y)\\le r_{N}\\). For large \\(m_{0}\\), \\(q\\in\\mathcal A\\) and\n\\[\nd(hq,hy)\\le C_{E}r_{N}/R,\n\\]\nso \\(\\omega_{\\xi}\\) occurs in \\(\\sigma_{q}\\).</p><p>Some level point lies within \\(r_{N}/4\\) of \\(y\\), hence in \\(\\overline B(q,2r_{N})\\). Thus \\(t\\) lies in the closed range interval \\(I_{q}\\) of \\(H_{N}\\) on that ball. Accounting for all target cells gives\n\\[\n1\\le\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal A}\\sigma_{q}\\mathbf1_{I_{q}}(t)\n\\qquad(t\\in I).\n\\]\nIntegration and Cauchy–Schwarz yield\n\\[\n\\Delta\n\\le\nCM(N-l)^{1/2}\n\\left(\\sum_{q\\in\\mathcal A}|I_{q}|^{2}\\right)^{1/2}.\n\\]</p><p>Apply (3.4) with \\(J=2\\), together with its bounded-overlap summation. Taking\n\\[\nC(2)r_{N}&lt;R/4\n\\]\nconfines every charged center to\n\\[\nR/4\\le d(q,x)\\le4R,\n\\]\nproving (3.5).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Finite-cell level comparison, original Lemma 3.8.", "statement": "<p>Two comparison tools: original Lemmas 3.8 and 3.9.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Z\\) and the covers \\(T_{n}\\) are as in Propositions 1 and 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Finite-cell level comparison, original Lemma 3.8. Let \\(f,w\\) be continuous on an open set \\(V\\subset Z\\), let \\(K\\subset V\\) be compact, and let \\(I\\subset\\mathbb R\\) be an interval. Suppose that, for every \\(t\\in I\\), there is a continuum \\(D_{t}\\subset K\\) with\n   \\[\n   f=t\\text{ on }D_{t},\n   \\qquad\n   \\operatorname{osc}_{D_{t}}w\\ge b_{0}&gt;0.\n   \\]\n   For sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n   \\[\n   b_{0}|I|\n   \\le\n   \\sum_{\\substack{q\\in T_{n}\\\\B(q,2r_{n})\\cap K\\neq\\varnothing}}\n   \\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,2r_{n})}f\\,\n   \\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,2r_{n})}w.\n   \\tag{3.7}\n   \\]\n   Consequently these hypotheses cannot hold for sequences with both local oscillation energies on \\(K\\) tending to zero, if \\(b_{0}\\) and \\(|I|\\) stay bounded below by positive constants.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Stopping a continuum at a sphere, original Lemma 3.9. Let \\(D\\) be a continuum, \\(p\\in Z\\), and \\(r&gt;0\\). If\n   \\[\n   x\\in D\\setminus\\overline B(p,r),\n   \\qquad\n   D\\cap B(p,r)\\neq\\varnothing,\n   \\]\n   then \\(D\\) contains a subcontinuum through \\(x\\) meeting the radius-\\(r\\) sphere and avoiding \\(B(p,r)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For the finite-cell comparison, let \\(I_{q}(f)\\) be the closed range interval of \\(f\\) on the closed cell. For large \\(n\\), these cells lie in \\(V\\).</p><p>The interval \\(w(D_{t})\\) is covered by the range intervals of \\(w\\) on cells meeting \\(D_{t}\\), and each such cell has \\(t\\in I_{q}(f)\\). Therefore\n\\[\nb_{0}\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\substack{q:B(q,2r_{n})\\cap K\\neq\\varnothing}}\n\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,2r_{n})}w\\,\n\\mathbf1_{I_{q}(f)}(t).\n\\]\nIntegrate over \\(I\\), using\n\\[\n|I\\cap I_{q}(f)|\n\\le\n\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,2r_{n})}f.\n\\]\nOnly finitely many interval indicators are integrated, so no measurable selection of \\(D_{t}\\) is required. Cauchy–Schwarz gives the consequence about vanishing energies.</p><p>For the stopping assertion, first recall that, in a compact metric space \\(X\\), a point’s component is the intersection \\(Q_{x}\\) of its clopen neighborhoods.</p><p>To verify connectedness of \\(Q_{x}\\), suppose it is separated into two compact pieces in disjoint open sets \\(O_{1},O_{2}\\). Compactness of\n\\[\nX\\setminus(O_{1}\\cup O_{2})\n\\]\ngives a finite intersection \\(W\\) of clopen neighborhoods of \\(x\\) contained in their union. Its intersection with the \\(O_{i}\\) containing \\(x\\) is clopen in \\(X\\) and misses the other piece of \\(Q_{x}\\), a contradiction. The same compactness argument shows that every open neighborhood of a component contains a clopen neighborhood of it.</p><p>Apply this to\n\\[\nX=D\\setminus B(p,r)\n\\]\nand the component \\(C\\) of \\(x\\). If \\(C\\) missed the sphere, it would have a clopen neighborhood \\(W\\) in \\(X\\) missing the sphere. Compactness places \\(W\\) a positive distance from \\(\\overline B(p,r)\\), so \\(W\\) is also clopen in \\(D\\). It is nonempty and misses \\(D\\cap B(p,r)\\), contradicting connectedness. Thus \\(C\\) is the required subcontinuum.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "exclusion of exponential records", "depth": 0, "snippet": "This contradiction setup is impossible.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the unbounded-modulus setting of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume additionally that the record parameter satisfies \\(\\lambda&gt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u\\) be the nonconstant locally Hölder scalar limit from Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Scalar approximation indices belong to the subsequence retained there.</p><p class=\"li\">For a continuous function \\(F\\), let \\(\\mathcal N(F)\\) be the points of its domain at which it is not locally constant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This contradiction setup is impossible. In particular, unbounded moduli cannot satisfy\n\\[\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}M(n)^{1/n}&gt;1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemmas 5.1 and 5.2 to obtain \\(W\\) on \\(Z\\setminus\\{b\\}\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\nE=\\operatorname{Stab}_{G}(b)\n\\]\nand let \\(\\Lambda_{E}\\) be its at-most-two accumulation directions, supplied by Lemma 5.4.</p><p>Choose a variation region \\(O\\) for \\(W\\) whose closure avoids\n\\[\n\\{b\\}\\cup\\Lambda_{E}.\n\\]\nSuch a region exists: otherwise \\(W\\) would be locally constant on the connected sphere minus this finite set, and continuity would make it constant throughout its domain.</p><p>Apply Lemma 5.1 to \\(W\\) on \\(Z\\setminus\\{b\\}\\), with this \\(O\\) and \\(z_{0}=b\\). Write the expansions as\n\\[\ns_{i}=v_{i}^{-1},\n\\]\nwith centers \\(y_{i}\\in O\\), depths \\(\\ell_{i}\\to\\infty\\), and punctures\n\\[\np_{i}=s_{i}b\\to b_{*}.\n\\]\nPositive affine normalizations of\n\\[\nW_{i}=W\\circ s_{i}^{-1}\n\\]\nconverge locally uniformly off \\(b_{*}\\) to a continuous nonconstant \\(W_{*}\\).</p><p>The punctures have a pairwise distinct subsequence. Indeed, if\n\\[\nv_{i}^{-1}b=v^{-1}b\n\\]\ninfinitely often, then \\(v_{i}v^{-1}\\in E\\). After a subsequence, \\(y_{i}\\to y\\in\\overline O\\). Since \\(v_{i}\\) lies at depth \\(\\ell_{i}\\) on a ray to \\(y_{i}\\), the prefix estimates give \\(v_{i}\\to y\\) in boundary direction. Bounded right multiplication preserves this direction, implying \\(y\\in\\Lambda_{E}\\), a contradiction.</p><p>Choose two coordinate disks \\(D_{1},D_{2}\\), with compact closures off \\(b_{*}\\), having disjoint \\(W_{*}\\)-range intervals and each containing a compact variation path. To do this, follow a variation path to its first hitting points of two distinct intermediate values. Just before either first hitting time the values differ, so the function is not locally constant at either point. Continuity supplies disks with disjoint ranges, and non-local constancy supplies a variation path in each.</p><p>For every sufficiently large \\(i\\), \\(p_{i}\\) is outside the closed disks. Uniform convergence of the normalized \\(W_{i}\\) preserves both the disjoint range intervals and the positive variation on the two fixed paths. Positive affine normalization preserves these properties, so \\(\\mathcal N(W_{i})\\) meets each disk.</p><p>Fix two distinct such indices \\(i,j\\). By Lemma 5.3,\n\\[\nW_{j}(x)=W_{j}(p_{i})\n\\]\nat every point \\(x\\in\\mathcal N(W_{i})\\) in either disk. Taking one point in each contradicts the disjoint \\(W_{j}\\)-range intervals. Thus \\(\\lambda&gt;1\\) is impossible.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1 (Hölder magnification)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are depths \\(k_{i}\\to\\infty\\), centers \\(x_{i}\\in O\\), positive numbers \\(\\sigma_{i}\\), and expansions \\( h_{i}=v_{i}^{-1} \\) at \\((x_{i},k_{i})\\), such that, after passage to a subsequence …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(Z\\) has the boundary geometry of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(f\\) is a real-valued, locally Hölder continuous function on an open set \\(\\Omega\\subset Z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(O\\) is open, \\(\\overline O\\subset\\Omega\\), and \\(O\\) contains a compact path on which \\(f\\) is nonconstant.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(z_{0}\\) with \\(\\operatorname{dist}(z_{0},O)&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are depths \\(k_{i}\\to\\infty\\), centers \\(x_{i}\\in O\\), positive numbers \\(\\sigma_{i}\\), and expansions\n\\[\nh_{i}=v_{i}^{-1}\n\\]\nat \\((x_{i},k_{i})\\), such that, after passage to a subsequence,\n\\[\np_{i}=h_{i}z_{0}\\longrightarrow b,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(x_{i},Z\\setminus O)}{r_{k_{i}}}\n\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]\nEvery compact subset of \\(Z\\setminus\\{b\\}\\) eventually lies in \\(h_{i}\\Omega\\), and\n\\[\nF_{i}(z)\n=\n\\frac{f(h_{i}^{-1}z)-f(x_{i})}{\\sigma_{i}}\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nconverges locally uniformly there to a nonconstant locally Hölder continuous function \\(F\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Write\n\\[\n\\delta(x)=\\operatorname{dist}(x,Z\\setminus O)\n\\]\nand fix \\(\\eta&gt;Q\\). A finite cover of \\(\\overline O\\) gives a uniform Hölder bound, with some exponent \\(\\alpha_{0}&gt;0\\), on a neighborhood of \\(\\overline O\\).</p><p>For all large \\(k\\), put\n\\[\nA_{k}\n=\n\\sup_{x\\in O}\n\\delta(x)^{\\eta}\n\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(x,r_{k})}f.\n\\]\nThe balls lie in \\(\\Omega\\), and\n\\[\nca^{-Qk}\\le A_{k}\\le Ca^{-\\alpha_{0}k}.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p><p>For the lower bound, cover a compact variation path \\(P\\subset O\\) by at most \\(Cr_{k}^{-Q}\\) radius-\\(r_{k}\\) balls centered on \\(P\\), using packing. Their range intervals cover the interval \\(f(P)\\), so one ball has oscillation at least \\(cr_{k}^{Q}\\); also \\(\\min_{P}\\delta&gt;0\\). The upper bound is the Hölder estimate.</p><p>Fix \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;\\alpha_{0}\\). Since \\(A_{k}a^{\\alpha k}\\to0\\), there are arbitrarily late indices attaining their maximum over the remaining tail. At these indices,\n\\[\nA_{k+j}\\le a^{-\\alpha j}A_{k}\n\\qquad(j\\ge0).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(x_{k}\\in O\\) attaining at least half the supremum, and put\n\\[\n\\delta_{k}=\\delta(x_{k}),\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_{k}=A_{k}/\\delta_{k}^{\\eta}.\n\\]\nBoundedness of \\(f\\) and (4.2) give\n\\[\n\\delta_{k}^{\\eta}\\ge cr_{k}^{Q},\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\n\\delta_{k}/r_{k}\n\\ge\ncr_{k}^{Q/\\eta-1}\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Expand at \\((x_{k},k)\\), and take a subsequence with\n\\[\np_{k}=h_{k}z_{0}\\to b.\n\\]\nFor compact \\(K\\subset Z\\setminus\\{b\\}\\), the puncture estimate gives\n\\[\nh_{k}^{-1}K\n\\subset\nB(x_{k},C_{K}r_{k})\n\\subset O\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\neventually.</p><p>The expansion formula gives\n\\[\nd(h_{k}^{-1}z,h_{k}^{-1}w)\n\\le\nC_{K}r_{k}d(z,w)\n\\]\non \\(K\\). On every fixed multiple of this source ball, \\(\\delta\\) is comparable to \\(\\delta_{k}\\). Thus (4.3) implies\n\\[\n\\frac{|f(y)-f(y')|}{\\sigma_{k}}\n\\le\nCa^{-\\alpha j}\n\\quad\\text{if }d(y,y')\\le r_{k+j},\n\\qquad j\\ge0.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nFor sufficiently small \\(d(z,w)\\), choosing \\(j\\ge0\\) with\n\\[\na^{-j}\\ge C_{K}d(z,w)&gt;a^{-j-1}\n\\]\nproves a uniform local \\(\\alpha\\)-Hölder estimate for \\(F_{k}\\) on \\(K\\).</p><p>For boundedness, the controlled-path estimate joins \\(h_{k}^{-1}z\\) to \\(x_{k}\\) inside a fixed multiple of \\(B(x_{k},r_{k})\\). Packing covers this path by a bounded number of radius-\\(r_{k}\\) balls centered on the path, each with oscillation at most \\(C\\sigma_{k}\\). Their range intervals cover the path’s image, so\n\\[\n|F_{k}(z)|\\le C_{K}.\n\\]</p><p>Compact exhaustion and Arzelà–Ascoli give a locally uniform, locally Hölder limit. Finally,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{h_{k}\\overline B(x_{k},r_{k})}F_{k}\\ge\\frac12.\n\\]\nThe lower puncture bound places all these image balls in one compact set off \\(b\\), so local uniform convergence makes the limit nonconstant.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 (Diagonal approximants)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After thinning the expansions, there are increasing scalar indices \\(N_{j}\\) such that \\( f_{j}(z) = \\frac{H_{N_{j}}(h_{j}^{-1}z)-u(x_{j})}{\\sigma_{j}}, \\qquad z\\in h_{j}\\widehat Q, \\) converges locally uniformly to …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Apply Lemma 5.1 to \\(u\\) on \\(U\\), taking a small variation region \\(O\\) and \\(z_{0}\\) outside \\(\\overline O\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Call the limit \\(W\\), its puncture \\(b\\), and retain \\(x_{i},k_{i},h_{i},\\sigma_{i}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After thinning the expansions, there are increasing scalar indices \\(N_{j}\\) such that\n\\[\nf_{j}(z)\n=\n\\frac{H_{N_{j}}(h_{j}^{-1}z)-u(x_{j})}{\\sigma_{j}},\n\\qquad z\\in h_{j}\\widehat Q,\n\\]\nconverges locally uniformly to \\(W\\) on \\(Z\\setminus\\{b\\}\\).</p><p>For every fixed \\(g\\in G\\), compact \\(K\\subset Z\\setminus\\{gb\\}\\), and fixed \\(J\\ge2\\),\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{N_{j}}^{(J)}(f_{j}\\circ g^{-1};K)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nAll indicated enlarged balls eventually lie in the functions’ domains.</p><p>If a compact path \\(P\\subset Z\\setminus\\{b\\}\\) has endpoint values of \\(W\\) strictly straddling a compact positive-length interval \\(I\\), then, for all large \\(j\\) and every \\(t\\in I\\), there is a continuum \\(D_{j,t}\\subset h_{j}\\widehat Q\\) with\n\\[\nf_{j}=t\\text{ on }D_{j,t},\n\\qquad\nD_{j,t}\\cap P\\neq\\varnothing,\n\\qquad\n\\sup_{t\\in I}\\operatorname{dist}(b,D_{j,t})\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(j\\ge4\\), put\n\\[\nK_{j}=\\{d(z,b)\\ge1/j\\},\n\\qquad\nL_{j}=\\{d(z,b)\\ge1/(2j)\\}.\n\\]\nChoose the \\(j\\)th expansion so that\n\\[\nh_{j}^{-1}L_{j}\\subset U\n\\]\nand its normalized \\(u\\)-transform differs from \\(W\\) by at most \\(1/j\\) on \\(L_{j}\\).</p><p>Hold this expansion and its normalization fixed. Choose \\(N_{j}&gt;N_{j-1}\\) so large that\n\\[\n\\sup_{L_{j}}\n\\frac{|H_{N_{j}}(h_{j}^{-1}z)-u(h_{j}^{-1}z)|}{\\sigma_{j}}\n\\le\\frac1j,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{N_{j}}^{(j)}(f_{j};K_{j})\\le\\frac1j,\n\\qquad\n(j+2)r_{N_{j}}&lt;\\frac1{2j}.\n\\]\nUniform approximation follows from Proposition 3. With \\(h_{j},\\sigma_{j}\\) fixed, its fixed-transform estimate bounds the displayed energy by\n\\[\n\\frac{C(h_{j},K_{j},j)}{\\sigma_{j}^{2}M(N_{j})},\n\\]\nso the energy condition follows by increasing \\(N_{j}\\). The last condition keeps all enlarged balls in \\(L_{j}\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(g\\), choose a compact neighborhood \\(K'\\) of \\(g^{-1}K\\) avoiding \\(b\\). Pulling back each target ball and choosing a nearest source net point gives, by bi-Lipschitz distortion and packing,\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{n}^{(J)}(F\\circ g^{-1};K)\n\\le\nC_{g}\\mathcal E_{n}^{(J')}(F;K')\n\\]\nfor some fixed \\(J'\\ge2\\) and all large \\(n\\). Each source center receives only boundedly many target centers. Eventually \\(K'\\subset K_{j}\\) and \\(J'\\le j\\), proving (4.6).</p><p>Uniform convergence makes the endpoint values of \\(f_{j}\\) straddle \\(I\\). The source level\n\\[\n\\tau_{j}(t)=u(x_{j})+\\sigma_{j}t\n\\]\ntherefore lies in \\((0,1)\\), and the separating-level assertion in Proposition 3’s construction supplies\n\\[\nD_{j,t}=h_{j}C_{N_{j},\\tau_{j}(t)}\n\\]\nmeeting \\(P\\).</p><p>Its source continuum has diameter at least \\(d_{1}\\), so contains a point at distance at least \\(d_{1}/2\\) from \\(x_{j}\\). By (2.3),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(b,D_{j,t})\n\\le\nd(b,p_{j})+Cr_{k_{j}}\n\\longrightarrow0\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(t\\in I\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.3 (Transform comparison)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If \\( c=gb\\neq b, \\qquad V=W\\circ g^{-1}, \\) then \\( V(x)=V(b) \\qquad \\bigl(x\\in\\mathcal N(W)\\setminus\\{c\\}\\bigr). \\) More generally, for \\( W_{s}=W\\circ s^{-1}, \\qquad p_{s}=sb, \\) if \\(p_{s}\\neq p_{t}\\), then …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(W,b,f_{j}\\) are as in Lemma 5.2.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal N(F)\\) denotes the points where a continuous function \\(F\\) is not locally constant.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\nc=gb\\neq b,\n\\qquad\nV=W\\circ g^{-1},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\nV(x)=V(b)\n\\qquad\n\\bigl(x\\in\\mathcal N(W)\\setminus\\{c\\}\\bigr).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p><p>More generally, for\n\\[\nW_{s}=W\\circ s^{-1},\n\\qquad\np_{s}=sb,\n\\]\nif \\(p_{s}\\neq p_{t}\\), then\n\\[\nW_{t}(x)=W_{t}(p_{s})\n\\qquad\n\\bigl(x\\in\\mathcal N(W_{s})\\setminus\\{p_{t}\\}\\bigr).\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose\n\\[\nx\\in\\mathcal N(W)\\setminus\\{c\\},\n\\qquad\nV(x)\\neq V(b).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\) and a coordinate disk about \\(x\\), compactly contained off \\(b,c\\), on which\n\\[\n|V-V(b)|\\ge4\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nIt contains a compact variation path \\(P\\) for \\(W\\).</p><p>Choose a compact positive-length interval \\(I\\) strictly between its endpoint values, avoiding \\(W(c)\\). Continuity gives \\(\\epsilon_{c}&gt;0\\) such that \\(\\overline B(c,\\epsilon_{c})\\) avoids \\(b\\) and its \\(W\\)-range has positive distance from \\(I\\). Consequently, for all large \\(j\\), the continua \\(D_{j,t}\\) of Lemma 5.2 avoid this closed ball.</p><p>Choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) so that \\(\\overline B(b,\\rho)\\) avoids \\(c,P\\), and\n\\[\n|V-V(b)|\\le\\varepsilon\n\\]\nthere. For\n\\[\nw_{j}=f_{j}\\circ g^{-1},\n\\]\nlocal uniform convergence gives\n\\[\n|w_{j}-V(b)|\\ge3\\varepsilon\\quad\\text{on }P,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|w_{j}-V(b)|\\le2\\varepsilon\n\\quad\\text{on }\\overline B(b,\\rho)\n\\]\nfor all large \\(j\\).</p><p>Each \\(D_{j,t}\\) meets \\(P\\) and enters \\(B(b,\\rho)\\). By the stopping assertion of Proposition 4, it has a subcontinuum joining a chosen point of \\(P\\) to the radius-\\(\\rho\\) sphere and contained in\n\\[\nK=\\{z:d(z,b)\\ge\\rho,\\ d(z,c)\\ge\\epsilon_{c}\\}.\n\\]\nThis subcontinuum lies in the level \\(f_{j}=t\\) and has \\(w_{j}\\)-oscillation at least \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>A fixed compact neighborhood of \\(K\\) avoiding both punctures eventually lies in\n\\[\nh_{j}U\\cap gh_{j}U\n\\]\nand contains every closed radius-\\(2r_{N_{j}}\\) cell meeting \\(K\\). Hence the finite-cell comparison of Proposition 4 and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\varepsilon|I|\n\\le\n\\bigl(\\mathcal E_{N_{j}}^{(2)}(f_{j};K)\\bigr)^{1/2}\n\\bigl(\\mathcal E_{N_{j}}^{(2)}(w_{j};K)\\bigr)^{1/2}\n\\longrightarrow0,\n\\]\na contradiction. All sets and \\(g\\) are fixed in this limit.</p><p>Finally apply (4.7) at \\(s^{-1}x\\), with group element \\(s^{-1}t\\), to obtain (4.8).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.4 (Stabilizer directions)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The vertices of \\(E\\) have at most two accumulation directions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">\\(G,Z\\) are as in Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">An accumulation direction of vertices going to infinity is a subsequential boundary limit.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(b\\in Z\\), put\n  \\[\n  E=\\operatorname{Stab}_{G}(b).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vertices of \\(E\\) have at most two accumulation directions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\xi\\) be a based ray to \\(b\\). For \\(q\\in E\\), \\(|q|\\le t\\), thinness of the ideal triangle with sides\n\\[\n[e,q],\\qquad \\xi,\\qquad q\\xi\n\\]\nputs \\(q\\xi(n)\\) uniformly near \\(\\xi\\) when \\(n\\) is sufficiently larger than \\(t\\). The nearby parameter lies in\n\\[\n[n-t-C,n+t+C].\n\\]\nFor one common integer \\(n\\), these vertices are distinct as \\(q\\) varies. Bounded valence therefore gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{q\\in E:|q|\\le t\\}\\le C'(t+1).\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]</p><p>If another accumulation direction exists, take\n\\[\nq_{n}\\to x\\neq b,\n\\qquad\nq_{n}^{-1}\\to y\n\\]\nalong a subsequence. Base change gives, uniformly on compact sets avoiding \\(y\\),\n\\[\n(q_{n}|q_{n}z)_{e}\n=\n|q_{n}|-(q_{n}^{-1}|z)_{e}+O(1)\n\\ge\n|q_{n}|-O(1).\n\\]\nBy the prefix estimates, \\(q_{n}z\\to x\\) there, and \\(q_{n}b=b\\) forces \\(y=b\\).</p><p>Choose disjoint small neighborhoods of \\(b,x\\), with a point outside their union. For large \\(n\\), \\(q=q_{n}\\) sends the complement of the first into the second. Its positive iterates never return the outside point, so \\(q\\) has infinite order. It is therefore loxodromic, with endpoints \\(b,x'\\); see [9, III.\\(\\Gamma\\).3.10].</p><p>If some \\(h\\in E\\) moved \\(x'\\), the cyclic subgroups generated by \\(q\\) and \\(hqh^{-1}\\) would intersect trivially: a nonzero common power would have the same pair of endpoints. Their \\(m^{2}\\) products\n\\[\nq^{i}(hqh^{-1})^{j},\n\\qquad 1\\le i,j\\le m,\n\\]\nwould then be distinct and have length \\(O(m)\\), contradicting (4.9).</p><p>Thus every element of \\(E\\) fixes both endpoints. It maps a geodesic line between them to a line at uniformly bounded Hausdorff distance, so its orbit of \\(e\\) stays a fixed distance from that line. Its only possible accumulation directions are the two endpoints.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.4 (Properties of the flow)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a fixed sufficiently large \\(D\\), a root depth \\(k_{0}\\), and a tree of such nodes with the following properties.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(M(n)\\) is unbounded and\n  \\[\n  \\limsup_{j\\to\\infty}M(j)^{1/j}\\le1.\n  \\tag{5.1}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u,\\mu,C_{t},d_{1}\\) be given by Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(x_{0}\\in U\\) where \\(u\\) is not locally constant, and \\(\\epsilon_{0}&gt;0\\) with\n  \\[\n  \\overline B(x_{0},3\\epsilon_{0})\\subset U.\n  \\]\n  Put\n  \\[\n  K=\\overline B(x_{0},\\epsilon_{0}),\n  \\qquad\n  K^{+}=\\overline B(x_{0},2\\epsilon_{0}).\n  \\]\n  Shrink \\(\\epsilon_{0}\\), if necessary, and choose \\(z_{0}\\) a positive distance from \\(K^{+}\\). Use the constants of (3.6) for this fixed region.</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(c_{\\mathrm{in}}=2L\\). Choose \\(c_{0}&gt;c_{\\mathrm{in}}+2\\) so large that, with\n  \\[\n  A=4Lc_{0}+20,\n  \\qquad\n  B_{0}=A+10,\n  \\qquad\n  c_{\\mathrm{pat}}=\\frac{2C_{E}c_{\\mathrm{in}}}{(A-2)^{2}},\n  \\]\n  one has\n  \\[\n  \\frac{c_{E}c_{0}}{(B_{0}+2)^{2}}&gt;12c_{\\mathrm{pat}}.\n  \\tag{5.10}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose\n  \\[\n  H&gt;\\max\\{4(B_{0}+1),\\,4C_{\\mu}c_{0},\\,2\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a positive integer subdivision depth, to be chosen sufficiently large, and put\n  \\[\n  \\theta=a^{-D}.\n  \\]\n  Require \\(\\theta&lt;1/4\\) and \\(c_{0}\\theta&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A node is a center \\(x\\in K\\), a scale \\(R=r_{k}\\), and a nonempty open interval\n  \\[\n  J=(j^{-},j^{+})\\subset(0,1)\n  \\]\n  such that\n  \\[\n  J\\subset\n  \\left(\n  \\min_{\\overline B(x,c_{0}R)}u,\\,\n  \\max_{\\overline B(x,c_{0}R)}u\n  \\right).\n  \\tag{5.2}\n  \\]\n  Write \\(\\Delta=|J|\\) and \\(r=\\theta R\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All node scales are small enough that the fixed source enlargements lie in \\(K^{+}\\), satisfy (3.6), and obey\n  \\[\n  (B_{0}+2)R&lt;d_{1}/2.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a node, choose a maximal \\(r\\)-separated set \\(Y\\) in\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal A_{x}(R)\n  =\n  \\{y:(A-1)R\\le d(x,y)\\le(B_{0}+1)R\\}.\n  \\]\n  Packing gives\n  \\[\n  \\#Y\\le C_{Y}a^{QD},\n  \\tag{5.3}\n  \\]\n  with \\(C_{Y}\\) independent of \\(D\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(y\\in Y\\), let \\(I_{y}^{\\mathrm{in}}\\) and \\(I_{y}^{\\mathrm{out}}\\) be the closed intervals between the extrema of \\(u\\) on\n  \\[\n  \\overline B(y,2r),\n  \\qquad\n  \\overline B(y,c_{0}r),\n  \\]\n  respectively. Put\n  \\[\n  J_{y}=J\\cap\\operatorname{int}I_{y}^{\\mathrm{out}},\n  \\qquad\n  t_{y}=|J_{y}|/\\Delta.\n  \\]\n  Then\n  \\[\n  |J\\cap I_{y}^{\\mathrm{out}}|=t_{y}\\Delta,\n  \\tag{5.4}\n  \\]\n  including singleton ranges.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  0&lt;c_{*}\\le\\frac1{8\\max\\{C_{Y},1\\}},\n  \\qquad\n  \\kappa=c_{*}a^{-QD}.\n  \\]\n  A node is good if two candidates \\(y^{-},y^{+}\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  |I_{y^{-}}^{\\mathrm{in}}\\cap(j^{-},j^{-}+\\Delta/4)|\n  \\ge\\kappa\\Delta,\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  |I_{y^{+}}^{\\mathrm{in}}\\cap(j^{-}+3\\Delta/4,j^{+})|\n  \\ge\\kappa\\Delta,\n  \\tag{5.5}\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{dist}\n  (I_{y^{-}}^{\\mathrm{out}},I_{y^{+}}^{\\mathrm{out}})\n  \\ge\\Delta/4.\n  \\tag{5.6}\n  \\]\n  Goodness uses the full candidate set.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix\n  \\[\n  b_{*}=\\frac12,\n  \\qquad\n  \\tau=\\frac1{20},\n  \\qquad\n  \\beta=\\frac{\\tau}{4(Q+1)},\n  \\qquad\n  \\gamma=\\frac{\\beta}{2}.\n  \\tag{5.7}\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a fixed sufficiently large \\(D\\), a root depth \\(k_{0}\\), and a tree of such nodes with the following properties.</p><p>The root is centered at \\(x_{0}\\), has scale \\(R_{0}=r_{k_{0}}\\), and carries a nonempty interval \\(J_{0}\\) of length \\(\\Delta_{0}&gt;0\\). Retained children are \\(Hr\\)-separated and have nested nonempty intervals \\(J_{y}\\subset J\\). Their transition probabilities \\(p_{y}\\) sum to one and satisfy:\n\\[\np_{y}\\le a^{-\\gamma D}t_{y},\n\\qquad\np_{y}\\le a^{-b_{*}D}t_{y}^{2}\n\\quad\\text{at nodes that are not good},\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\np_{y}\\le a^{-\\gamma D}t_{y},\n\\qquad\np_{y}\\le a^{\\tau D}t_{y}^{2}\n\\quad\\text{at good nodes}.\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]</p><p>Generation-\\(n\\) centers are \\(Hr_{k_{0}+Dn}\\)-separated. Descendants of a node of scale \\(R\\) stay within\n\\[\n\\frac{B_{0}+1}{1-\\theta}R\\le2(B_{0}+1)R\n\\tag{5.19}\n\\]\nof its center.</p><p>Let \\(P_{i}\\) be the product of transition probabilities to node \\(i\\), let \\(\\Delta_{i}\\) be its interval length, and, for a generation-\\(n\\) node, let \\(m_{i}\\) count its non-good ancestors in generations \\(0,\\ldots,n-1\\). Then\n\\[\nP_{i}\\le a^{-\\gamma Dn}\\frac{\\Delta_{i}}{\\Delta_{0}},\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_{i}^{2}\n\\ge\n\\Delta_{0}^{2}P_{i}\na^{D((b_{*}+\\tau)m_{i}-\\tau n)}.\n\\tag{5.21}\n\\]\nIf \\(i\\) lies \\(m\\) generations below \\(j\\), then\n\\[\n\\frac{P_{i}}{P_{j}}\n\\le\na^{-\\gamma Dm}\\frac{\\Delta_{i}}{\\Delta_{j}}\n\\le\na^{-\\gamma Dm}.\n\\tag{5.22}\n\\]\nInfinitely many generations have total probability at least \\(1/2\\) on good nodes.</p><p>Every node has a fixed expansion. Each good node has a pattern from a finite list of ordered target-ball pairs. Under its expansion, each target ball contains a path with endpoint variation at least \\(\\kappa\\Delta_{i}\\), the whole-ball range intervals have distance at least \\(\\Delta_{i}/4\\), and the balls lie in the images of the respective outer source balls of radius\n\\[\nc_{0}\\theta r_{k_{0}+Dn}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The choice of \\(c_{0}\\) is possible because the ratio of the left side of (5.10) to its right side grows linearly in \\(c_{0}\\). All subsequent geometric choices are fixed before choosing \\(D\\).</p><p>The uniform upper bound on node scales can be chosen before \\(D\\): all source radius factors are fixed, and the subsidiary scales \\(r,s\\) never exceed \\(R\\). Thus source neighborhoods and measure enlargements stay in \\(K^{+}\\).</p><p>At a node that is not good, put uniform probabilities on the candidates from Lemma 6.1. For large \\(D\\), they satisfy (5.8), since\n\\[\np_{y}\\le c_{H}^{-1}a^{-D},\n\\qquad\nt_{y}\\ge1/8,\n\\qquad\n\\gamma,b_{*}&lt;1.\n\\]</p><p>Fix \\(D\\) large enough also for Lemma 6.2. At each good node, divide the positive capacities on its separated set \\(Y'\\) by their sum. The resulting probabilities satisfy (5.9). Every positive transition retains a nonempty open child interval \\(J_{y}\\subset J\\).</p><p>Fix a node’s expansion \\(h=v^{-1}\\) when constructing it. At a good node, join the extrema of each inner ball by a path in\n\\[\nB(y^{\\pm},c_{\\mathrm{in}}r).\n\\]\nEach path has endpoint variation at least \\(\\kappa\\Delta\\). Its points lie at distance at least \\((A-2)R\\) from \\(x\\), so (2.2) puts its image within \\(c_{\\mathrm{pat}}\\theta/2\\) of \\(hy^{\\pm}\\).</p><p>Conversely, write\n\\[\nD_{w}=\\max\\{R,d(x,w)\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(d(y,z)\\ge c_{0}r\\), then\n\\[\nD_{z}\\le D_{y}+d(y,z).\n\\]\nMonotonicity of \\(s/(D_{y}+s)\\) gives\n\\[\nd(hy,hz)\n\\ge\n\\frac{c_{E}Rc_{0}r}{D_{y}(D_{y}+c_{0}r)}\n\\ge\n\\frac{c_{E}c_{0}\\theta}{(B_{0}+2)^{2}}\n&gt;\n12c_{\\mathrm{pat}}\\theta.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\overline B(hy,10c_{\\mathrm{pat}}\\theta)\n\\subset h(B(y,c_{0}r)).\n\\tag{5.18}\n\\]</p><p>Choose a fixed finite \\(c_{\\mathrm{pat}}\\theta\\)-net in \\(Z\\). For each \\(hy^{\\pm}\\), choose a net point within \\(c_{\\mathrm{pat}}\\theta\\), and take the closed radius-\\(3c_{\\mathrm{pat}}\\theta\\) ball about it. The ordered pair\n\\[\n(\\mathcal B^{-},\\mathcal B^{+})\n\\]\nis the node’s pattern.</p><p>Each ball contains its path image and lies in the corresponding outer source-ball image. For \\(u\\circ h^{-1}\\), the two whole-ball range intervals have distance at least \\(\\Delta/4\\), and each ball contains a path with endpoint variation at least \\(\\kappa\\Delta\\). There are finitely many patterns, independently of depth.</p><p>Choose \\(k_{0}\\) so large that \\(R_{0}=r_{k_{0}}\\) satisfies all source-scale conditions and\n\\[\n2(B_{0}+1)R_{0}&lt;\\epsilon_{0}/2,\n\\qquad\n(B_{0}+2)R_{0}&lt;d_{1}/2.\n\\]\nNon-local constancy at \\(x_{0}\\) supplies a nonempty open interval\n\\[\nJ_{0}\\subset(0,1)\n\\]\nstrictly inside the range on \\(\\overline B(x_{0},c_{0}R_{0})\\). Construct the tree recursively, storing each node’s expansion and, at good nodes, its pattern.</p><p>A child moves by at most \\((B_{0}+1)R\\) from a parent of scale \\(R\\). Consequently all descendants stay within\n\\[\n\\frac{B_{0}+1}{1-\\theta}R\\le2(B_{0}+1)R\n\\]\nof that parent, and all centers lie in \\(K\\).</p><p>Generation \\(n\\) has scale \\(r_{k_{0}+Dn}\\), and its centers are \\(H\\) times that scale apart. This holds for siblings; children of distinct parents of scale \\(R\\) are separated by at least\n\\[\n[H-2(B_{0}+1)]R&gt;HR/2&gt;H\\theta R.\n\\]</p><p>At any level of scale \\(R\\), (3.6) gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_{i}^{2}\n\\le\nC\\mu\\bigl(\\overline B(x_{i},C_{\\mu}c_{0}R)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThese enlarged balls are disjoint because \\(H&gt;4C_{\\mu}c_{0}\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{i}\\Delta_{i}^{2}\\le E_{0},\n\\qquad\nE_{0}=C\\mu(Z)&lt;\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Give the root probability one. Every level has total probability one. Multiplying the linear and quadratic bounds along a branch, and telescoping interval ratios, gives (5.21). The same linear calculation conditionally below a node gives (5.22).</p><p>Lemma 6.3 now supplies infinitely many levels with good mass at least \\(1/2\\). The preceding construction proves all stated geometric and probabilistic properties.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 5.1 (Many children at a node that is not good)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(D\\), the node has at least \\( c_{H}a^{D} \\) mutually \\(Hr\\)-separated candidates with \\(t_{y}\\ge1/8\\), where \\(c_{H}>0\\) is independent of the node and \\(D\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the node, candidate, interval, and geometric definitions of Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">The node is not good.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(D\\), the node has at least\n\\[\nc_{H}a^{D}\n\\]\nmutually \\(Hr\\)-separated candidates with \\(t_{y}\\ge1/8\\), where \\(c_{H}&gt;0\\) is independent of the node and \\(D\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For every \\(t\\in J\\), a path in \\(B(x,Lc_{0}R)\\) strictly straddles \\(t\\) and hence meets \\(C_{t}\\). Since \\(\\operatorname{diam}C_{t}\\ge d_{1}\\), the continuum leaves \\(\\overline B(x,(B_{0}+2)R)\\) and meets every intervening radial sphere.</p><p>Fix \\(b\\in[AR,B_{0}R]\\). Each \\(C_{t}\\), \\(t\\in J\\), meets the radius-\\(b\\) sphere, so the inner intervals of candidates with\n\\[\n|d(x,y)-b|\\le r\n\\]\ncover \\(J\\). By (5.3), two of them meet the respective quarters in (5.5) by at least \\(\\kappa\\Delta\\).</p><p>If both have \\(t_{y}&lt;1/8\\), the lower-quarter outer interval ends below \\(j^{-}+3\\Delta/8\\), and the upper-quarter interval starts above \\(j^{-}+5\\Delta/8\\). Indeed, each meets its quarter but overlaps \\(J\\) in length less than \\(\\Delta/8\\); the assertion also holds for intervals extending beyond \\(J\\).</p><p>Their distance exceeds \\(\\Delta/4\\), making the node good. Otherwise one candidate has \\(t_{y}\\ge1/8\\).</p><p>Apply this at radial values spaced by \\((H+3)r\\). There are at least \\(c_{H}a^{D}\\) such values, and the resulting centers are more than \\(Hr\\) apart.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemma 5.2 (Separated capacity)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large \\(D\\), uniformly over all nodes, there is an \\(Hr\\)-separated subset \\(Y'\\subset Y\\) such that \\( \\sum_{y\\in Y'} \\min\\{a^{-\\gamma D}t_{y},\\,a^{\\tau D}t_{y}^{2}\\} \\ge1. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the full node setting of Proposition 6, including the subexponential condition (5.1).</p><p class=\"li\">The node need not be good.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large \\(D\\), uniformly over all nodes, there is an \\(Hr\\)-separated subset \\(Y'\\subset Y\\) such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{y\\in Y'}\n\\min\\{a^{-\\gamma D}t_{y},\\,a^{\\tau D}t_{y}^{2}\\}\n\\ge1.\n\\tag{5.11}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Join two candidates in a graph when their distance is at most \\(Hr\\). Packing bounds its degree, so graph coloring partitions \\(Y\\) into at most \\(C_{H}\\) separated classes, with \\(C_{H}\\) independent of \\(D\\).</p><p>If the conclusion fails, then\n\\[\n\\sum_{y\\in Y}\n\\min\\{a^{-\\gamma D}t_{y},\\,a^{\\tau D}t_{y}^{2}\\}\n&lt;C_{H}.\n\\tag{5.12}\n\\]</p><p>Call \\(y\\) large when\n\\[\nt_{y}\\ge a^{-(\\tau+\\gamma)D}\n\\]\nand small otherwise. On large candidates the minimum is linear, so\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{large }y}t_{y}\\le C_{H}a^{\\gamma D}.\n\\tag{5.13}\n\\]</p><p>Set\n\\[\nD'=\\lceil\\beta D\\rceil,\n\\qquad\ns=a^{-D'}R.\n\\]\nFor large \\(D\\),\n\\[\n0&lt;D'&lt;D,\n\\qquad\ns&lt;R/4.\n\\]\nChoose \\(s\\)-spaced radial bins \\(\\mathcal B\\) in\n\\[\n[(A+1)R,(B_{0}-1)R],\n\\]\nso\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal B\\asymp a^{D'}.\n\\]</p><p>On \\(\\mathcal B\\times J\\), use counting measure times Lebesgue measure. Mark \\((b,t)\\) if a large candidate satisfies\n\\[\n|d(x,y)-b|\\le5s,\n\\qquad\nt\\in I_{y}^{\\mathrm{out}}.\n\\]\nEach candidate meets at most eleven bins. By (5.4) and (5.13), the marked measure is at most\n\\[\nCC_{H}a^{\\gamma D}\\Delta.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\gamma&lt;\\beta\\), the unmarked measure is at least\n\\[\nca^{D'}\\Delta.\n\\tag{5.14}\n\\]</p><p>Take a maximal \\(s\\)-separated set \\(\\mathcal Z_{s}\\) in \\(\\mathcal A_{x}(R)\\). Its radius-\\(s\\) balls cover the annulus, and\n\\[\n\\#\\mathcal Z_{s}\\le Ca^{QD'}.\n\\]\nExpand at each \\(z\\in\\mathcal Z_{s}\\), at depth \\(k+D'\\), by \\(h_{z}\\). Put\n\\[\nq=r/s=a^{-(D-D')},\n\\]\nand take macroscopic admissible weights \\(\\omega_{\\xi}\\), \\(\\xi\\in T_{D-D'}\\), of squared mass \\(M(D-D')\\).</p><p>For fixed \\(C_{T}&gt;2+C_{E}\\), define\n\\[\n\\sigma_{y}^{z}\n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\n\\sum_{\\xi:d(\\xi,h_{z}y)\\le C_{T}q}\\omega_{\\xi},\n&amp;d(y,z)\\le3s,\\\\{}[1ex]\n0,&amp;d(y,z)&gt;3s,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\sigma_{y}=\\sum_{z\\in\\mathcal Z_{s}}\\sigma_{y}^{z}.\n\\]</p><p>Within \\(\\overline B(z,3s)\\), the candidate images are separated by a fixed multiple of \\(q\\). Thus every sum has boundedly many terms and every target weight is charged boundedly many times. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_{y}(\\sigma_{y}^{z})^{2}\\le CM(D-D')\n\\]\nfor each \\(z\\).</p><p>Also each \\(y\\) lies within \\(3s\\) of boundedly many auxiliary centers. Packing and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\\[\n\\sum_{y}\\sigma_{y}^{2}\n\\le\nCa^{QD'}M(D-D').\n\\tag{5.15}\n\\]</p><p>Fix an unmarked \\((b,t)\\). Choose \\(w\\in C_{t}\\) on the radius-\\(b\\) sphere and \\(z\\in\\mathcal Z_{s}\\) with \\(d(w,z)\\le s\\).</p><p>The diameter bound gives a point of \\(C_{t}\\) outside \\(\\overline B(w,2s)\\). Join these points in the \\(r/4\\)-neighborhood of \\(C_{t}\\), using Proposition 1, and stop at the first radius-\\(s\\) hit about \\(w\\). The resulting path \\(\\pi\\) has diameter at least \\(s\\) and lies in\n\\[\n\\overline B(w,s)\\subset\\overline B(z,2s).\n\\]\nThe scale-\\(s\\) expansion \\(h_{z}\\) makes it macroscopic.</p><p>The radial bin lies between \\((A+1)R\\) and \\((B_{0}-1)R\\), while \\(s&lt;R/4\\). Thus every point of \\(\\pi\\) lies between radii\n\\[\n(A+3/4)R,\\qquad(B_{0}-3/4)R\n\\]\nabout \\(x\\). In particular, it lies in the candidate annulus and has a candidate center within distance \\(r\\).</p><p>The same fixed source margin places every level point within \\(r/4\\) of \\(\\pi\\) in \\(K^{+}\\subset U\\). On these points Proposition 3 gives \\(u=t\\), even though it does not assert this identity on parts of \\(C_{t}\\) outside \\(U\\).</p><p>For a target cell meeting \\(h_{z}\\pi\\), choose a preimage hit \\(v\\in\\pi\\) and \\(y\\in Y\\) with \\(d(y,v)\\le r\\). Then \\(d(y,z)&lt;3s\\). If \\(\\xi\\) is the target cell’s center, (2.2) gives\n\\[\nd(\\xi,h_{z}y)&lt;(2+C_{E})q&lt;C_{T}q,\n\\]\nso its weight occurs in \\(\\sigma_{y}^{z}\\).</p><p>A point of \\(C_{t}\\) is within \\(r/4\\) of \\(v\\), hence within \\(5r/4&lt;c_{0}r\\) of \\(y\\). The preceding interior check gives\n\\[\nt\\in I_{y}^{\\mathrm{out}}.\n\\]\nSince\n\\[\n|d(x,y)-b|\\le s+r&lt;5s,\n\\]\nunmarkedness makes \\(y\\) small. Admissibility yields\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\text{small }y\\\\|d(x,y)-b|\\le5s}}\n\\sigma_{y}\\mathbf1_{I_{y}^{\\mathrm{out}}}(t)\n\\ge1\n\\tag{5.16}\n\\]\non every unmarked pair.</p><p>Integrating (5.16), using bounded bin multiplicity, gives\n\\[\nca^{D'}\\Delta\n\\le\nC\\Delta\\sum_{\\text{small }y}\\sigma_{y}t_{y}.\n\\]\nOnly finitely many closed-interval indicators are integrated; no measurable selection of continua or paths is needed, and singleton ranges contribute zero.</p><p>Cancel \\(\\Delta&gt;0\\). Cauchy–Schwarz and (5.15) imply\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{small }y}t_{y}^{2}\n\\ge\n\\frac{ca^{-(Q-2)D'}}{M(D-D')}.\n\\tag{5.17}\n\\]</p><p>Subexponential growth gives\n\\[\nM(D-D')\\le a^{\\tau D/4}\n\\]\nfor large \\(D\\): first bound\n\\[\nM(j)\\le Ca^{\\tau j/8}\n\\]\nand then absorb \\(C\\) by increasing \\(D\\).</p><p>Since\n\\[\nD'\\le\\beta D+1,\n\\qquad\n(Q-2)\\beta&lt;\\tau/4,\n\\]\none gets\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\text{small }y}a^{\\tau D}t_{y}^{2}\n\\ge\nca^{[3\\tau/4-(Q-2)\\beta]D}\n\\ge\nca^{\\tau D/2}.\n\\]\nOn small candidates this is exactly the capacity, contradicting (5.12). Every threshold for \\(D\\) is uniform over nodes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 5.3 (Levels with substantial good mass)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Infinitely many generations have total probability at least \\(1/2\\) on good nodes.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider a rooted probability tree of nodes as in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">The root interval has length \\(\\Delta_{0}&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Transition probabilities obey (5.8) and (5.9), and node intervals are nested.</p><p class=\"li\">At every level, suppose\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{i}\\Delta_{i}^{2}\\le E_{0}&lt;\\infty.\n  \\tag{5.20}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a generation-\\(n\\) node \\(i\\), let \\(P_{i}\\) be its probability and \\(m_{i}\\) its number of non-good ancestors in generations \\(0,\\ldots,n-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Infinitely many generations have total probability at least \\(1/2\\) on good nodes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiplying the quadratic transition bounds and telescoping interval ratios gives\n\\[\n\\Delta_{i}^{2}\n\\ge\n\\Delta_{0}^{2}P_{i}\na^{D((b_{*}+\\tau)m_{i}-\\tau n)}.\n\\]\nBy (5.20) and Jensen’s inequality,\n\\[\n\\frac{E_{0}}{\\Delta_{0}^{2}}\n\\ge\n\\sum_{i}P_{i}\na^{D((b_{*}+\\tau)m_{i}-\\tau n)}\n\\ge\na^{D((b_{*}+\\tau)\\sum_{i}P_{i}m_{i}-\\tau n)}.\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\n\\frac1n\\sum_{i}P_{i}m_{i}\n\\le\n\\frac{\\tau}{b_{*}+\\tau}+O(1/n)\n=\n\\frac1{11}+O(1/n).\n\\]</p><p>Conservation of probability identifies the left side with the average bad mass over the first \\(n\\) generations. If good mass were eventually less than \\(1/2\\), that average would have lower limit at least \\(1/2\\), a contradiction.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "exclusion of unbounded subexponential moduli", "depth": 0, "snippet": "These assumptions are impossible.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the boundary and modulus setting of Propositions 1 and 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(M(n)\\) is unbounded and\n  \\[\n  \\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}M(n)^{1/n}\\le1.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the flow of Proposition 6, including \\(D,k_{0},H,\\kappa,\\Delta_{0}\\), and a compact neighborhood of its centers in \\(U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the point \\(z_{0}\\) from the flow construction, a positive distance from that neighborhood.</p><p class=\"li\">At generation \\(n\\), put\n  \\[\n  k=k_{0}+Dn,\n  \\qquad\n  R=a^{-k},\n  \\qquad\n  p_{i}=h_{i}z_{0},\n  \\qquad\n  h_{i}=v_{i}^{-1}.\n  \\]\n  The centers \\(x_{i}\\) are \\(HR\\)-separated.</p><p class=\"li\">Use arbitrarily large generations with good mass at least \\(1/2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A good node has a pattern \\((\\mathcal B^{-},\\mathcal B^{+})\\) from a fixed finite list. Each ball contains a compact path with endpoint \\(u\\circ h_{i}^{-1}\\)-variation at least \\(\\kappa\\Delta_{i}\\), and the two target range intervals have distance at least \\(\\Delta_{i}/4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">All geometric and counting constants below are independent of the generation, nodes, and scalar approximation index \\(N\\). Convergence thresholds may depend on a pair after it is fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These assumptions are impossible. Thus the moduli cannot be unbounded while satisfying\n\\[\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}M(n)^{1/n}\\le1.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Assign each unordered far pair from Lemma 7.2 to a qualifying endpoint supplied by Lemma 7.3.</p><p>At any point, (6.12) allows only \\(Ca^{Ql}\\) paying indices \\(i\\), and (6.11), together with Lemma 7.1, allows boundedly many partners for each. Integrating the multiplicity bound of Lemma 7.4 gives\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{\\{i,j\\}\\text{ far}\\\\\\text{assigned to }i}}\n\\mu(D_{ij})\n\\le\nCa^{Ql}\\mu(Z).\n\\tag{6.13}\n\\]\nThis counts the enlarged balls directly and uses no invariance of \\(\\mu\\). The assignment may be arbitrary when both endpoints qualify: all lower bounds hold for either endpoint, and the multiplicity estimate already counts every possible ordered contribution.</p><p>Conversely, Proposition 6’s linear probability bound gives, at every paying endpoint,\n\\[\n\\Delta_{i}^{2}\n\\ge\n\\Delta_{0}^{2}a^{2\\gamma Dn}P_{i}^{2}\n\\ge\n\\Delta_{0}^{2}a^{2\\gamma Dn}P_{*}^{2}.\n\\]\nThere are at least \\(cN_{\\mathcal I}^{2}\\) far pairs. By (6.10) and (6.3), the total cost is at least\n\\[\ncN_{\\mathcal I}^{2}\\kappa^{2}\\Delta_{0}^{2}\na^{2\\gamma Dn}P_{*}^{2}\n\\ge\nc'k^{-2}a^{2\\gamma Dn}.\n\\tag{6.14}\n\\]</p><p>Since \\(\\kappa,\\Delta_{0}&gt;0\\) are fixed and\n\\[\nQl=QD\\lfloor\\gamma n/Q\\rfloor\\le\\gamma Dn,\n\\]\ncomparison with (6.13) yields\n\\[\na^{\\gamma Dn}\\le Ck^{2}.\n\\]\nThis contradicts \\(k=k_{0}+Dn\\), excluding the unbounded subexponential case.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.2 (Bounded puncture multiplicity)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every fixed \\(C>0\\), the balls \\( \\{\\overline B(p_{i},CR):i\\text{ at generation }n\\} \\) have uniformly bounded multiplicity.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the generation-\\(n\\) nodes, common depth \\(k\\), scale \\(R\\), and punctures \\(p_{i}\\) of Proposition 7.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every fixed \\(C&gt;0\\), the balls\n\\[\n\\{\\overline B(p_{i},CR):i\\text{ at generation }n\\}\n\\]\nhave uniformly bounded multiplicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Centers assigned to the same depth-\\(k\\) vertex have mutual distance \\(O(R)\\). Their \\(HR\\)-separation and packing bound their number.</p><p>If several puncture balls share a point, their punctures have mutual products at least\n\\[\nk-O_{C}(1).\n\\]\nBy (2.4) and the prefix estimates, all corresponding inverse vertices lie in one bounded Cayley-graph ball. There are boundedly many such vertices, each with the bounded multiplicity just proved.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemma 6.3 (Probability near a puncture), together with the probability-class selection preceding it", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The class \\(\\mathcal I\\) can be chosen as above, and \\(N_{\\mathcal I}\\to\\infty\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use a sufficiently large generation from Proposition 7.</p><p class=\"li\">Discard nodes with \\(P_{i}&lt;a^{-2Qk}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Partition the remaining good nodes by pattern and into dyadic probability classes.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a class \\(\\mathcal I\\) and \\(P_{*}&gt;0\\) such that\n  \\[\n  P_{*}\\le P_{i}\\le2P_{*}\\qquad(i\\in\\mathcal I),\n  \\qquad\n  \\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal I}P_{i}\\ge c/k.\n  \\tag{6.2}\n  \\]\n  Write \\(N_{\\mathcal I}=|\\mathcal I|\\), so\n  \\[\n  N_{\\mathcal I}P_{*}\\ge c/(2k).\n  \\tag{6.3}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\zeta=\\gamma/Q,\n  \\qquad\n  m=\\lfloor\\zeta n\\rfloor,\n  \\qquad\n  l=Dm.\n  \\]\n  Both \\(l\\) and \\(k-l\\) tend to infinity, and \\(Ql\\le\\gamma Dn\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The class \\(\\mathcal I\\) can be chosen as above, and \\(N_{\\mathcal I}\\to\\infty\\).</p><p>For every generation-\\(n\\) node \\(i\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{j\\text{ at generation }n\\\\d(p_{j},p_{i})\\le a^{-l}}}\nP_{j}\n\\le\nCa^{-\\gamma Dm}.\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]</p><p>Consequently, at least \\(cN_{\\mathcal I}^{2}\\) unordered pairs in \\(\\mathcal I\\) satisfy\n\\[\nd_{ij}:=d(p_{i},p_{j})&gt;a^{-l}.\n\\tag{6.5}\n\\]\nCall these far pairs. Uniformly for far pairs,\n\\[\nR/d_{ij}\\le a^{-(k-l)}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>There are at most \\(Ca^{Qk}\\) nodes. Discarding those with\n\\[\nP_{i}&lt;a^{-2Qk}\n\\]\nloses probability at most \\(Ca^{-Qk}\\), so the remaining good nodes have mass at least \\(1/3\\) for large \\(n\\).</p><p>There are finitely many patterns and \\(O(k)\\) dyadic probability classes. One class therefore satisfies (6.2). Its size satisfies (6.3).</p><p>Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\nP_{i}\\le a^{-\\gamma Dn},\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nN_{\\mathcal I}\\ge ck^{-1}a^{\\gamma Dn}\\longrightarrow\\infty.\n\\]</p><p>Fix an ancestor \\(A_{0}\\) at generation \\(n-m\\), whose depth is\n\\[\nk-l=k_{0}+D(n-m).\n\\]\nOnly boundedly many descendants \\(j\\) of \\(A_{0}\\) can satisfy\n\\[\nd(p_{j},p_{i})\\le a^{-l}.\n\\]</p><p>To see this, split a geodesic word\n\\[\nv_{j}^{-1}=w_{j}u'_{j},\n\\qquad\n|w_{j}|=l,\n\\qquad\n|u'_{j}|=k-l.\n\\]\nThe puncture condition and (2.4) give\n\\[\n(p_{j}|p_{i})_{e}\\ge l-C,\n\\qquad\n(v_{j}^{-1}|p_{j})_{e}\\ge k-C.\n\\]\nThus \\(w_{j}\\) lies a bounded distance from the depth-\\(l\\) point on a fixed ray to \\(p_{i}\\), leaving boundedly many possibilities.</p><p>Reversing and inverting the same word gives the geodesic\n\\[\nv_{j}=(u'_{j})^{-1}w_{j}^{-1}.\n\\]\nIts depth-\\((k-l)\\) prefix is \\((u'_{j})^{-1}\\).</p><p>Since the descendant displacement estimate gives\n\\[\nd(x_{j},x_{A_{0}})\n\\le\n2(B_{0}+1)r_{k-l},\n\\]\nthinness and the prefix estimates put this prefix a bounded distance from the depth-\\((k-l)\\) point on a fixed ray to \\(x_{A_{0}}\\). Hence \\(u'_{j}\\) also has boundedly many possibilities.</p><p>Their products determine boundedly many vertices \\(v_{j}\\), and the first step of Lemma 7.1 bounds the nodes assigned to each. This counts group elements, so nonuniqueness of geodesic spellings causes no additional multiplicity. The source and inverse prefixes together account for the full word.</p><p>Each such descendant satisfies\n\\[\nP_{j}\\le P_{A_{0}}a^{-\\gamma Dm}.\n\\]\nSum first over these descendants and then over ancestors, whose probabilities sum to one. This proves (6.4).</p><p>For each \\(i\\in\\mathcal I\\), the fraction of its class with puncture distance at most \\(a^{-l}\\) is bounded by\n\\[\n\\frac{Ca^{-\\gamma Dm}}{N_{\\mathcal I}P_{*}}\n\\le\nCka^{-\\gamma Dm}\n=o(1).\n\\]\nThis includes the diagonal. Hence at least \\(cN_{\\mathcal I}^{2}\\) unordered pairs are far.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.4 (Pair alternative)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every far pair at a sufficiently large generation has an endpoint \\(i\\) satisfying \\( \\operatorname{osc}_{h_{i}^{-1}E_{j}}u \\ge \\frac{\\kappa}{16}\\Delta_{i}. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the common-pattern class \\(\\mathcal I\\) and far pairs of Lemma 7.2.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_{c}\\subset U\\) contain all source centers, with its closed \\(\\epsilon\\)-neighborhood still in \\(U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(A_{h}\\) so that, for the puncture-bound constant \\(C\\),\n  \\[\n  2C/A_{h}&lt;\\epsilon/2,\n  \\qquad\n  A_{h}/2&gt;2C/d_{1}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  E_{i}=\\overline B(p_{i},A_{h}R).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The upper puncture estimate gives\n  \\[\n  d(h_{i}y,p_{i})\\ge A_{h}R/2\n  \\Longrightarrow\n  d(x_{i},y)\\le D_{y}\\le2C/A_{h}.\n  \\tag{6.6}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Every \\(h_{i}C_{N,t}\\) reaches \\(B(p_{i},A_{h}R/2)\\). The target pattern balls stay a uniform positive distance from every \\(p_{i}\\) in the class.</p><p class=\"li\">At sufficiently large generations, far pairs have disjoint \\(E_{i},E_{j}\\), each compactly contained in the other chart’s domain, and their shared pattern balls avoid both puncture balls.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every far pair at a sufficiently large generation has an endpoint \\(i\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{h_{i}^{-1}E_{j}}u\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\kappa}{16}\\Delta_{i}.\n\\tag{6.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The preliminary domain and separation assertions follow as follows. A source continuum of diameter at least \\(d_{1}\\) has a point at distance at least \\(d_{1}/2\\) from \\(x_{i}\\), whose image has puncture distance at most \\(2CR/d_{1}\\). Thus \\(h_{i}C_{N,t}\\) reaches the inner puncture ball.</p><p>The preimages of the pattern balls lie in the outer source balls, where\n\\[\nD_{y}\\le(B_{0}+2)R.\n\\]\nThe lower puncture bound therefore gives a uniform target distance \\(\\delta_{*}&gt;0\\) from \\(p_{i}\\). The pattern is shared, so this holds for every \\(i\\in\\mathcal I\\). For large \\(n\\), uniformly for far pairs,\n\\[\n2A_{h}R&lt;a^{-l},\n\\qquad\nA_{h}R&lt;\\delta_{*}/2.\n\\]\nThe asserted common domains follow from (6.6).</p><p>Write\n\\[\nF_{i}=u\\circ h_{i}^{-1},\n\\qquad\nF_{j}=u\\circ h_{j}^{-1}\n\\]\non their open domains.</p><p>If\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{E_{i}}F_{j}\\ge\\Delta_{j}/16,\n\\]\nendpoint \\(j\\) qualifies, using \\(\\kappa\\le1\\).</p><p>Otherwise one pattern ball \\(\\mathcal B^{s}\\) has its \\(F_{j}\\)-range interval at distance at least \\(\\Delta_{j}/16\\) from that on \\(E_{i}\\). Indeed, if neither did, the two pattern intervals would have distance less than \\(3\\Delta_{j}/16\\), contradicting separation by \\(\\Delta_{j}/4\\).</p><p>Node \\(i\\) supplies a compact path\n\\[\n\\eta\\subset\\mathcal B^{s}\n\\]\nwith endpoint \\(F_{i}\\)-gap at least \\(\\kappa\\Delta_{i}\\).</p><p>Suppose\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{E_{j}}F_{i}&lt;\\kappa\\Delta_{i}/4.\n\\]\nRemoving this latter range interval from the endpoint gap leaves a positive-length closed interval\n\\[\nI\\subset(0,1)\n\\]\nstrictly between the endpoint values and a positive distance from the range on \\(E_{j}\\). Choose a smaller closed interval inside one of the remaining open intervals to obtain positive margins. Its length need not be uniform over pairs.</p><p>Fix the pair and \\(I\\) before letting \\(N\\to\\infty\\). Put\n\\[\nF_{i,N}=H_{N}\\circ h_{i}^{-1},\n\\qquad\nF_{j,N}=H_{N}\\circ h_{j}^{-1}.\n\\]\nThe fixed range margins and local uniform convergence imply, for every \\(t\\in I\\) and all sufficiently large \\(N\\), that \\(h_{i}C_{N,t}\\) hits \\(\\eta\\) and avoids \\(E_{j}\\). It also reaches the inner \\(i\\)-puncture ball.</p><p>For the first assertion, endpoint values still strictly straddle all of \\(I\\), so the separating-level property applies to the preimage path. For the second, the range on \\(E_{j}\\) remains disjoint from \\(I\\).</p><p>By the stopping assertion of Proposition 4, the continuum has a subcontinuum \\(K_{N,t}\\) containing a hit on \\(\\eta\\), meeting the sphere\n\\[\nd(z,p_{i})=A_{h}R,\n\\]\nand avoiding \\(B(p_{i},A_{h}R)\\). It remains in the level \\(F_{i,N}=t\\) and avoids \\(E_{j}\\). In particular,\n\\[\nK_{N,t}\\subset\nK_{ij}\n:=\nZ\\setminus\n\\bigl(B(p_{i},A_{h}R)\\cup B(p_{j},A_{h}R)\\bigr)\n\\Subset h_{i}U\\cap h_{j}U.\n\\]\nCompact containment follows from (6.6) with half the radius. The sphere hit belongs to \\(E_{i}\\); no identification of that sphere with the boundary of a metric ball is needed.</p><p>Uniform convergence on \\(\\eta\\cup E_{i}\\) preserves the \\(F_{j}\\)-range gap, giving\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{K_{N,t}}F_{j,N}\\ge\\Delta_{j}/32\n\\]\nuniformly for \\(t\\in I\\).</p><p>The finite-cell comparison of Proposition 4 gives\n\\[\n\\frac{\\Delta_{j}}{32}|I|\n\\le\n\\sum_{\\substack{q\\in T_{N}\\\\B(q,2r_{N})\\cap K_{ij}\\neq\\varnothing}}\n\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,2r_{N})}F_{i,N}\\,\n\\operatorname{osc}_{\\overline B(q,2r_{N})}F_{j,N}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(N\\), these closed cells lie in the common domain. Both squared oscillation sums tend to zero by Proposition 3’s fixed-transform bounds. Cauchy–Schwarz contradicts the positive left side.</p><p>Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{osc}_{E_{j}}F_{i}\\ge\\kappa\\Delta_{i}/4,\n\\]\nproving the alternative. The argument applies separately to every pair above the geometric threshold; no convergence rate uniform over pairs is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For sufficiently large generations, \\( D_{z_{ij}}\\asymp R/d_{ij}, \\) and \\( h_{i}^{-1}E_{j}\\subset\\overline B(z_{ij},s_{ij}). \\) If endpoint \\(i\\) satisfies the pair alternative, then …", "statement": "<p>Cost balls and total multiplicity, from original Section 6.3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the class \\(\\mathcal I\\), far pairs, puncture balls, and pair alternative from Lemmas 7.2 and 7.3.</p><p class=\"li\">For an ordered far pair \\((i,j)\\), put\n  \\[\n  z_{ij}=h_{i}^{-1}p_{j}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a sufficiently large fixed \\(C_{2}\\), and define\n  \\[\n  s_{ij}=C_{2}R^{2}/d_{ij}^{2},\n  \\qquad\n  D_{ij}=\\overline B(z_{ij},C_{\\mu}s_{ij}),\n  \\]\n  using a uniform enlargement constant \\(C_{\\mu}\\) in (3.6) on a compact neighborhood of the centers.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For sufficiently large generations,\n\\[\nD_{z_{ij}}\\asymp R/d_{ij},\n\\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nh_{i}^{-1}E_{j}\\subset\\overline B(z_{ij},s_{ij}).\n\\tag{6.9}\n\\]\nIf endpoint \\(i\\) satisfies the pair alternative, then\n\\[\n\\mu(D_{ij})\\ge c\\kappa^{2}\\Delta_{i}^{2}.\n\\tag{6.10}\n\\]</p><p>There are fixed \\(C_{3},C_{4}\\) such that\n\\[\nh_{i}D_{ij}\\subset\\overline B(p_{j},C_{3}R),\n\\tag{6.11}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nD_{ij}\\subset\\overline B(x_{i},C_{4}Ra^{l}).\n\\tag{6.12}\n\\]\nConsequently the family of all ordered-pair balls \\(D_{ij}\\) has multiplicity at most \\(Ca^{Ql}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(y\\in h_{i}^{-1}E_{j}\\), then\n\\[\nd(h_{i}y,p_{i})\n\\ge\nd_{ij}-A_{h}R\n\\ge d_{ij}/2.\n\\]\nThe upper puncture bound gives\n\\[\nD_{y}\\le CR/d_{ij}.\n\\]\nThese points approach \\(x_{i}\\) uniformly, so the two-sided puncture bound applies and gives\n\\[\nD_{z_{ij}}\\asymp R/d_{ij}.\n\\]</p><p>The lower expansion bound and\n\\[\nd(h_{i}y,p_{j})\\le A_{h}R\n\\]\nthen give\n\\[\nd(y,z_{ij})\\le CR^{2}/d_{ij}^{2}.\n\\]\nFor sufficiently large \\(C_{2}\\), this proves (6.9).</p><p>The centers \\(z_{ij}\\) stay in the fixed compact neighborhood of the source centers, and the radii \\(s_{ij}\\) tend uniformly to zero. If endpoint \\(i\\) qualifies, (6.7), (6.9), and (3.6) give\n\\[\n\\mu(D_{ij})\\ge c\\kappa^{2}\\Delta_{i}^{2}.\n\\]</p><p>The enlarged balls retain small images. The function\n\\[\nD_{y}=\\max\\{R,d(x_{i},y)\\}\n\\]\nis \\(1\\)-Lipschitz, and\n\\[\n\\frac{C_{\\mu}s_{ij}}{D_{z_{ij}}}\n\\le\nC\\frac{R}{d_{ij}}\n\\le\nCa^{-(k-l)}\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThus \\(D_{y}\\asymp D_{z_{ij}}\\) on \\(D_{ij}\\). The upper expansion bound yields\n\\[\nh_{i}D_{ij}\\subset\\overline B(p_{j},C_{3}R).\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(i\\), Lemma 7.1 therefore bounds the multiplicity of \\(D_{ij}\\) as \\(j\\) varies.</p><p>Also\n\\[\nd(x_{i},z_{ij})\\le CR/d_{ij},\n\\]\nwhile the cost radius is \\(o(R/d_{ij})\\). Since \\(d_{ij}&gt;a^{-l}\\),\n\\[\nD_{ij}\\subset\\overline B(x_{i},C_{4}Ra^{l}).\n\\]\nThe \\(HR\\)-separation of the \\(x_{i}\\) and packing bound the multiplicity of these larger balls by \\(Ca^{Ql}\\). Combining the two counts gives the asserted total multiplicity.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [1, 2, "cite"], [3, 8, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 11, "cite"], [3, 16, "cite"], [3, 17, "cite"], [3, 18, "cite"], [3, 20, "cite"], [3, 23, "cite"], [3, 26, "cite"], [3, 28, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 3, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 3, "cite"], [7, 3, "cite"], [8, 0, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 18, "cite"], [8, 28, "cite"], [9, 8, "cite"], [10, 8, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"], [11, 19, "cite"], [11, 21, "cite"], [11, 24, "cite"], [11, 26, "cite"], [11, 31, "cite"], [12, 11, "cite"], [12, 14, "cite"], [13, 14, "cite"], [13, 15, "cite"], [14, 11, "cite"], [14, 15, "cite"], [14, 16, "cite"], [15, 11, "cite"], [15, 16, "cite"], [16, 11, "cite"], [17, 11, "cite"], [18, 22, "cite"], [18, 31, "cite"], [19, 0, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [20, 21, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [23, 19, "cite"], [24, 28, "cite"], [24, 30, "cite"], [25, 24, "cite"], [26, 24, "cite"], [27, 24, "cite"], [28, 0, "cite"], [29, 28, "cite"], [29, 30, "cite"], [29, 32, "cite"], [30, 28, "cite"], [30, 31, "cite"], [30, 32, "cite"], [31, 28, "cite"], [31, 32, "cite"], [32, 28, "cite"]]});
